v2.0.1:
    Fit the panadapter window's dropdowns to their values on Windows
       The panadapter window made its dropdowns without
       qt_fit_combobox_width, so on Windows Scale, Span and FPS were too
       narrow to show their values. Each is now fitted to its widest item,
       Fixed Tune once its items are added.
   
    Retry hdiutil when it fails to make the macOS .dmg
       On the macOS runners, hdiutil now and then fails with "Resource busy",
       something else briefly holding the image it is making, which failed
       the build after the app had been built; run again, it went through.
       It is now tried up to five times, a while apart, before the build
       fails.
   
    Add a GitHub workflow making a draft release for a version tag
       Pushing a tag v<version> starts Release, which checks that the tag is
       for the version in k4companion.yaml, runs the Windows, macOS and Linux
       build workflows, and puts what they build, with the manuals, in a draft
       GitHub release for the tag, to be looked over and published by hand. A
       version with a hyphen in it is marked a pre-release. The build
       workflows can now be called from another workflow for this, and can
       still be run on their own.
   
    Use the GitHub actions' Node.js 24 versions in the workflows
       checkout@v4, setup-python@v5 and upload-artifact@v4 run on Node.js 20,
       which GitHub has deprecated, warning of it at every build. Their v7s
       run on Node.js 24, and change nothing these workflows use.
   
    Build the Linux installer for ARM on Ubuntu 24.04
       PyQt6's wheels for ARM need glibc 2.39, which Ubuntu 22.04 doesn't
       have, so pip tried to build PyQt6 from source there and failed. The ARM
       installer is now built on Ubuntu 24.04, and so runs on Ubuntu 24.04 or
       later, Debian 13 and the Raspberry Pi OS based on it, or Fedora 40 or
       later. The x86_64 one is still built on 22.04.
       
       Each installer is now built whether or not the other is, rather than
       one failing cancelling the other.
   
    Add a GitHub workflow building a Linux installer
       Build Linux makes k4companion-linux-x86_64.run and
       k4companion-linux-aarch64.run, self-extracting installers (made with
       makeself) holding the program PyInstaller builds and
       tools/linux/install.sh. Run as yourself, it installs in
       ~/.local/share/k4companion, with a k4companion link in ~/.local/bin
       and a desktop menu entry; run with sudo, in /opt/k4companion, adding
       the K-Pod's udev rule. Either way it leaves an uninstall script.
       
       The audio libraries, udev and the C++ runtime are left for the
       system's copies, which have to agree with its sound setup and graphics
       drivers; the installer says what to install where PortAudio or Opus is
       missing. Qt's GTK theme is left out, which brought GTK along, and with
       it the libraries nothing else in the bundle needs. Built on Ubuntu
       22.04, the oldest runner, so that it runs on that and newer.
   
    We support MIDI keyers, not only MoMIDI keyers

v2.0:
    Leave the version out of the installers' file names
       The Windows workflow's installer is now k4companion-installer.exe,
       as Windows/ holds it, and the macOS workflow's disk images
       k4companion-macos-arm64.dmg and k4companion-macos-x86_64.dmg. The
       version is still read, for the Windows uninstall entry and the Mac
       app's own version.
   
    Update the README, User Manual and Quick Start for version 2.0
       Covers the changes since 1.6.2: the Qt interface, the panadapter window,
       the keyer and its Keying tab, the local sidetone, the encrypted
       connection on port 9204, the moved NB and noise reduction controls, the
       Audio tab's exclusive mode and Remote Audio Latency, and the new
       dependencies. Every screenshot in the manual is now of the 2.0
       interface.
       
       Also adds the Writer sources of the User Manual and the Quick Start and
       the MacOS installation guide.
   
    Update Windows executables

    Show a splash screen while the Windows programs start
       Add tools/k4_splash.png as a PyInstaller splash screen to
       k4companion.exe and k4companion-debug.exe, shown while the .exe
       unpacks itself and Python imports Qt, until close_splash_screen()
       takes it down. The build installs Pillow, which PyInstaller needs to
       scale the image to fit 760x480.
       
       Start the panadapter subprocesses with
       PYINSTALLER_SUPPRESS_SPLASH_SCREEN set, so that a panadapter window
       doesn't bring the splash screen back.
   
    Widen the settings dropdowns on Windows to fit their values
       With the combobox stylesheet, Qt's sizeHint() comes up about 10px
       short on Windows, so the Audio tab's dropdowns clipped their longest
       device names. Add the same 10px that Dropdown_Widget already adds.
   
    Change the Audio Effects button into a dropdown

    Give the S-meters more room above them
       Leading 3 for each VFO's S-meter, where it was 1: a little more space
       between the frequency and the meter under it.
   
    Line up CMP with SWR
       CMP had leading 2 where the meters beside it have the default of 1,
       from the tkinter interface, and sat a few pixels below SWR in Qt.
   
    Keep a bargraph's left text from making it taller
       The left text beside a horizontal bar is a label in the meter's font
       with a margin above and below, which stood taller than a bar thinned
       by thicknesspercent, so a bargraph with any came out taller than one
       without: SWR, with a space of it, a few pixels taller than CMP beside
       it. Its frame is now kept to the bar's height, so it gives the bar
       its width and nothing else. All lefttext in the configuration is a
       space, which is never seen whatever is clipped.
   
    Remove the version from the installer filename

    Wake the keyer early by as much as its sleep has lately overrun
       The keyer woke 2 ms before a key down's deadline, which is all the
       sleep overruns by on Linux and Windows, but on macOS, whose timers
       are coalesced, it overran by 3 to 5 ms on most elements and missed
       the deadline every time, clipping each tone it then queued late.
       
       sleep_until now says how far it overran, and the keyer keeps the most
       of that it has lately seen, letting it go by 2% an element, and wakes
       that much earlier as well, by no more than 10 ms. In a simulation of
       held dits with every sleep 3 or 5 ms late, only the first key down,
       before anything had been learned, was queued late. The queued late
       debug line carries the figure.
   
    Keep a key down the keyer queued late from raising the sidetone lag
       On macOS the keyer's sleep before queueing a key down overran by 3 to
       5 ms on most elements, past the block's deadline every time, and
       next_key_edge added each shortfall to sidetone_lag. Every later
       element was then heard that much later, and the lag climbed a
       millisecond or two an element: 224 ms in 40 seconds of sending, far
       faster than ease_sidetone_lag gives it back.
       
       The keyer now says when it queues a key down after the deadline, and
       next_key_edge places that one as early as its block allows without
       raising the lag. That tone starts a little late and is that much
       shorter, its key up being queued from where it should have started,
       and nothing after it moves. paddle_sweep.py's stand-in for the audio
       carries the new sidetone_down_late.
   
    Leave the parts of Qt the program never uses out of the .exe files
       tools/k4companion.spec builds k4companion.exe and k4companion-debug.exe
       from one PyInstaller analysis, which the workflow now runs in place of
       its two command lines. It leaves out about 16 MB of each: software
       OpenGL, Qt's PDF library and image format plugins, Qt's TLS plugins
       and the OpenSSL they would load, and Qt's translations. The other Qt
       bindings are excluded too, so that pyqtgraph's hook can't pick them.
   
    Compress the installer as one block
       The installer compressed k4companion.exe and k4companion-debug.exe
       each on its own, so the nearly identical second file cost as much as
       the first. Solid LZMA with a 64 MB dictionary, larger than either
       file, stores the second almost for nothing: 118 MB down to 60 MB with
       the 2.1-rc0 programs, at the cost of about a minute to build.
   
    Install k4companion-debug.exe beside k4companion.exe
       k4companion.exe has no console, so -d and --da show nothing.
       k4companion-debug.exe is the same program built with one; the
       installer now installs it and the uninstaller removes it. It gets no
       shortcut, being for running from a Command Prompt. make windows needs
       Windows/k4companion-debug.exe as well.
   
    Stop installing k4companion.yaml
       K4 Companion's configuration is built in, so the file isn't needed.
       The uninstaller still removes it, as left by earlier versions.
   
    Rename MoMIDI to just MIDI
       We support MIDI-protocol keyers, not just those that support the
       MoMIDI spec.
   
    Find opus.dll beside k4companion.exe, not only in the current folder
       opuslib finds opus.dll by searching PATH, to which only the current
       folder was added, so it was found only when K4 Companion happened to
       start in the folder holding it, as the installer's shortcuts do. Now
       PyInstaller's unpacked bundle and the folder holding k4companion.exe
       (or the script) are searched first.
   
    Add additional space above the antenna selectors

    Revise Windows Main tab layout to resemble Linux

    Update To Do list

    Send every command after an app-only one
       send_cmds set whether to send, and whether a command was an adjustment,
       once for a whole call rather than for each command in it, so a command
       the app handles itself, !MACROSDISABLE say, kept every command after it
       in the same call from being sent to the K4. Each command now starts
       afresh.
   
    Update opus.dll to version 1.6.1

    Keep a removed server from being saved back
       Erasing a server's name removes its settings file, renames the files of
       the servers after it down, and restarts. The restart's clean-up saves
       window geometry through the removed server's settings, all still in
       memory, so whenever the main or Macros window had moved or changed size,
       the file was written back: the server reappeared, and the next server's
       file, by then renamed into its place, was overwritten. The Macros window
       changing size with its tabs made that the usual case. Once its file is
       removed, a server's settings are never saved again.
   
    Size the decoded K4 audio queue by duration
       The queue held two packets, but how long a packet is follows the K4's
       remote audio latency setting, from 20 ms at SL0 to 120 ms at SL7, so it
       held anything from 40 to 240 ms. The packets arrive in bursts, with gaps
       of over 100 ms between them, and at the short end the queue ran dry in
       every gap and overflowed in every burst after it: dropouts and skips
       several times a second.
       
       It now holds up to 240 ms with the key up, never less than two packets,
       which ordinary jitter would otherwise trim at every one; and two packets
       while the key is down, as before, so what follows an element is current.
       What is over is trimmed from the oldest audio a sample at a time rather
       than a packet at a time. The raw queue, only the hand-off to the
       decoder, is deep enough never to drop, and decoding waits for two
       packets to start, as it did before.
       
       The audio glitch report gives the decoded audio dropped in milliseconds,
       and raw packets dropped separately.
   
    Count dropped K4 audio packets under --da audio
       A packet of K4 audio thrown away because its queue was already full,
       raw or decoded, joins the audio before it to the audio after it. That
       is a click with no underflow or starvation to show for it, so it went
       uncounted. It is reported with the other audio glitches now. Packets
       are dropped on purpose while the key is down, the sidetone covering the
       receive audio then.
   
    Count blocks short of K4 audio under --da audio
       A block the K4's audio runs out partway through, with the key up, is
       padded with silence and faded back in. It is the K4's stream pausing
       rather than the callback being late, so PortAudio flags no underflow
       for it, and it went uncounted. It is reported with the other audio
       glitches now.
   
    Keep the dropdowns from holding the GIL at every response
       A dropdown's response handler took 3 to 6 ms, long enough to make the
       audio output callback underrun, the Att dropdown's at every RA; for
       instance:
       
       - It set the box's style sheet at every response matching a selection,
       and Qt restyles the box and its popup even for the same sheet. It is
       only set when it differs, and the text likewise.
       
       - A response matching no selection had unexpectedresponse evaluated
       twice.
       
       - eval_value compiled each config expression afresh at every call, once
       for each selection. Each is now compiled once, which every widget using
       an eval option gains from.
   
    Add --da audio to find what makes the audio output underrun
       Everything here only runs with --da audio, which now also gates the
       audio glitch report and the stream and sample rate messages that any
       debug level used to turn on, and the notice of a device that does not
       support a multiple of the K4's sample rate, which was printed every
       time.
       
       - Each underflow says how long after the previous one its callback came,
       against the block length, and how far it was from the last RX; the K4
       sent and from the monitor being unmuted.
       
       - A K4 response handler taking 2 ms or more is logged, with its thread's
       CPU time beside the wall time, since the wall time counts waiting for
       the GIL too.
       
       - A garbage collection taking 2 ms or more is logged with the thread it
       ran in, and the types of the garbage last sampled, functions and frames
       named, to find what makes the cycles.
       
       - The stall watchdog dumps every thread's stack at 25 ms instead of 100.
       
       - How many objects were frozen out of garbage collection at startup is
       logged.
   
    Keep the GIL free for the audio output callback
       The output callback is Python and only a few milliseconds deep, so
       anything else holding the GIL that long made it underrun, and it was
       happening every few seconds while sending, mostly around the K4 dropping
       back to receive. Each of these was found holding it:
       
       - opuslib's decode_float allocated a new ctypes array for every packet,
       four times the size it needed, and ctypes.cast left each in a reference
       cycle. Collections freed thousands of them at a time, up to twenty
       milliseconds. OpusFloatDecoder decodes into one buffer instead.
       
       - The switch interval is down from 5 ms to 1 ms, so the callback waits
       less behind whatever thread holds the GIL.
       
       - What is built at startup is frozen out of garbage collection once the
       K4's initial parameters are in, since Python 3.14's incremental
       collector otherwise goes back over it.
       
       - The equalizer refilled three comboboxes at every TX/RX change; they
       are left alone when their items are the same.
       
       - The meters laid their bars out again at every reading, and
       QProgressBar.setValue repainted there and then, taking the rest of the
       window's pending paints with it. The layout is redone only when the
       scale changes, and the paint is left to the event loop.
       
       - A togglebutton configured every matching selection in turn at every
       response, restyling the connect button and starting and stopping a blink
       timer at every PONG. Only the one shown is configured, and only when it
       changes, and a style sheet is only set when it differs.
   
    Remember whether Macros is enabled across restarts
       The enabled setting was saved whenever !MACROS changed, but closing
       the app disables the macros, which saved them as disabled every time.
       Nothing is saved while the Macros window is closing now, so the user's
       own choice is what the next start sees.
   
    simulations: Regenerate the stall summaries
       Since 2380d1e the monitor is muted with ML0000 only where it is not
       muted already. It still is when the T's burst starts after the S, so the
       stored summaries' second ML0000 is no longer sent. Nothing else changed:
       every stall still passes, and the plots are identical.
   
    Catch up the sidetone split between the element and the gap
       The catch-up fraction was the excess over the tolerable lag taken as a
       number, so 1.1 ms behind trimmed a 139 ms dah by only 0.15 ms.
       
       Now the excess is split proportionally to the element and gap lengths.
       
       Half and half after a dit, three quarters and a quarter after a dah, so
       the element and the gap are shortened by the same share, still no more
       than 3%.
   
    Fade the K4 audio back in evenly in dB from the key up
       The raised cosine brought most of the loudness in over its last few
       milliseconds, so loud band audio came back abruptly after every element.
       The fade is now even in decibels from -40 dB, over 4 ms, as long as the
       sidetone's fall.
       
       It also starts on the key up frame rather than at the start of the block
       the key comes up in, so the K4 audio comes back the same way after every
       element instead of partway up already by however far into its block the
       key came up.
   
    Keep K4's monitor sidetone muted longer
       We now keep the K4's monitor sidetone muted for 0.5 seconds after
       the last sidetone would have sounded from the K4. The extra half
       second is so that we don't have to immediately send a mute command
       while we're keying continuously.
   
    Version 2.0-rc30
       Print debug messages in a separate thread.
   
    Write the debug log on a thread of its own
       A write to stderr blocks while its reader stops reading, and with the
       MIDI callback and the keyer's thread printing between their steps, a
       stalled stderr stalled the paddles too. dprint now queues each line for
       a log_writer thread, which is drained at exit.
   
    Version 2.0-rc29
       Add a stall watchdog when debugging
       Log straight key MIDI events that arrive 3 ms or more late
       Only report late midi events when > 3 ms
       Mute K4's sidetone monitor when straight keying
       Remove the warning popup for a WPM too high for the sidetone lag
   
    Add a stall watchdog when debugging
       Dumps every thread's stack when the process stops running Python for
       100 ms or more, and logs when the stall ended and how long it lasted.
   
    Log straight key MIDI events that arrive 3 ms or more late

    Only report late midi events when > 3 ms
       They were reported when greater than 1 ms, 1 to 2 ms is normal.
   
    Mute K4's sidetone monitor when straight keying

    Remove the warning popup for a WPM too high for the sidetone lag

    Version 2.0-rc28

    simulations: Add the dah-first and stall sweeps
       dah-first closes the dah at time zero and the dit 4 to 40ms after it,
       either side of the first tone being heard, and checks the squeeze
       begins with the dah and alternates. All pass at 30 and 40 WPM; against
       the keyer before 026e3ec, six of each set began with a dit.
       
       stall sends an S and then a T through midi_message_callback as a MoMIDI
       device does, the time since its last event carried in the messages,
       and stalls the whole process from just before the S's dit is let go of
       until the moment swept, 200 to 600ms: what the device sends meanwhile
       reaches the keyer in order at the stall's end, and the keyer's thread
       wakes a millisecond after it. Each run is checked for the S, the 200ms
       gap and the T. All 101 at 30 WPM and 75 at 40 pass; against 3f12782,
       before readings were taken as of when they were due, the 51 stalls
       ending from the dah's closing on sent V.
       
       It also found the gap a stalled burst's first KZD carried running from
       the stall's end, fixed in the commit before this.
       
       The other sets are unchanged, every plot and summary. The README gives
       each new set its section and brings the rest up to them.
   
    Tell the K4 a stalled burst's gap from when its paddle closed
       A burst begun from a paddle standing closed, with nothing queued for it,
       took the gap its first KZD carries from when it was keyed. Where the
       process had stalled with the paddle closed, that is the stall's end, and
       the K4 was told the gap as this thread saw it rather than as the
       operator left it: the new stall sweep had an S and a T 200ms apart go
       out with KZD0206 to KZD0254 for stalls ending while the T's dah was
       still held.
       
       send_burst keeps when the paddle closed, and the gap runs to that, or to
       where auto spacing held the element off until if that is later -- never
       past the keyed start, so only the thread's lateness is left out. The
       same as missed_first_element already did for a paddle closed and let go
       of again.
   
    Begin a squeeze with the paddle closed first
       With both paddles closed and no element before, iambic always sent a
       dit first, whichever paddle the operator closed first -- though the dah
       closed first had already had its tone queued as a dah. Paddle times now
       say reliably which closed first (see midi_event_timestamp), so a burst
       begun with both closed begins with that one, as ultimatic already does,
       and alternates from there. A dit where they closed together.
       
       A squeeze begun with the dah 2ms ahead of the dit went dit dah dit dah
       and goes dah dit dah dit now. Every stored sweep is unchanged: the
       squeeze sweeps close both paddles together.
   
    Read the paddles as they stood when a reading was due
       A reading of the paddles was taken of them as they stood when this
       thread got to it. Where the whole process stalls, the paddles' events
       arrive together at the end of it, and the keyer wakes to find them all
       standing. Simulating rc27_linux.txt's stall through the MIDI path with
       the keyer waking after everything had arrived, the reading for the
       element after an S's last dit -- due a gap after it -- saw the dah the
       operator closed a character space later as a tap during that dit, and S
       and T went out together as V.
       
       paddle_timestamps gives the four as they stood at a given moment, from
       a short history of the events reported, and each reading is taken as of
       when it was due: confirm_element's on the grid as it was before a stall
       moved it up, since whether there is an element was a question for the
       moment it should have been asked, and the reading at the tone's start
       as of when wait_until_sidetone_heard meant it to be taken. The look a
       tap is measured from is that same moment.
       
       That leaves the dah no part of the S, and it had arrived, whole, while
       the keyer was still finishing the S: start_burst_sidetone leaves a
       paddle closing inside a burst to the keyer, and by then it was open
       again, so the T was not sent at all. missed_first_element begins a
       burst with a paddle closed after the reading that ended the last one
       was due, keyed now and its gap still told to the K4 from when it
       closed. The same window, a few milliseconds wide, lost a quick tap
       before any of this.
       
       All three orderings of the stall simulated now send S, KZD0200 and T.
       Every stored sweep is unchanged.
   
    Give Keyer.midi_delta_time a starting value
       It was set only by a MIDI message whose own delta was a millisecond or
       more, and the event delta line formats it whether or not keyer debugging
       is on. A first note carrying an embedded time, ahead of any such
       message, raised AttributeError in the MIDI callback.
   
    Time MIDI paddle events by the device's own deltas
       Paddle events were stamped when they reached the callback, and that is
       not always a millisecond after the paddle moved. rc27_linux.txt has the
       process stalled some 0.44s as an S ended: the dit's release and the next
       character's four events all arrived together, stamped within 0.3ms of
       one another where the device put them 200, 2, 6 and 38ms apart. The
       MIDI layer had them on time -- its deltas agree with the device's -- so
       the time was lost getting them to Python. Judged by arrival, a 6ms brush
       of the dit paddle 2ms into a dah counted as a dit closed during it, and
       N went out for T; and the K4 was told the gap as the keyer saw it,
       stall and all.
       
       midi_event_timestamp keeps the device's clock as the sum of its deltas
       and puts it on perf_counter by the least any event has lately arrived
       behind it, so a late delivery keeps the time it happened. It starts
       over from arrival after a pause of midi_reanchor_seconds, whose delta
       the device may not carry whole, or where an event would be put that far
       before it arrived. The paddle timestamps take that time, and so does the
       gap a burst's first KZD carries; the tone is still keyed on arrival, an
       edge asked for in the past being dragged and its shortfall carried into
       sidetone_lag. Serial paddles are unchanged: their deltas are per paddle,
       and taken on perf_counter as the port is read.
       
       The rc27 case simulated through the MIDI path went out as S, KZD0410, N
       before, and as S, KZD0200, T now. Every stored sweep is unchanged.
   
    Keep an element never sent out of the next burst's gap
       start_element moves the grid up to now where this thread was held off
       past an element's grid start, and end_element_timestamp moves with it.
       Where confirm_element then found nothing wanted, the burst ended with
       end_element_timestamp still moved up -- and that is what the next
       burst's first KZD measures its gap from.
       
       rc27_linux.txt caught it: the process stalled about 0.4s as an S ended,
       the keyer woke to look for a fourth dit that was not wanted, and the
       K4 was told KZD0047 before the next character where the operator had
       left some 200ms by the device's own count. The two characters would
       have run together on the air.
       
       Put both back where confirm_element finds no element, so the gap is
       measured from the end of the last element sent. A simulated 0.4s stall
       after an S reported 340ms of a 700ms gap before, and 700ms now. Every
       stored sweep is unchanged, plots and summaries alike.
   
    simulations: Regenerate the 30 WPM swap plots
       6 of the 21, 84 to 104ms, show the KZD for the third element a few
       milliseconds later than they did, from 39926dd, for the reason given
       in fa4ae59: at 30 WPM a key down lands exactly on a block boundary, and
       its deadline, reckoned afresh, now falls on the block the edge is
       really in. The reading the KZD waits on is taken that much later. The
       KZD still carries the gap keyed, the sidetone is unchanged, and so is
       the summary. The copies in 30wpm/ are the same files.
   
    simulations: Regenerate the 30 WPM squeeze plots
       99 of the 125 show the KZD for an element after a burst's first a few
       milliseconds later than they did, from 39926dd. The summaries, which
       record what was sent but not when, are unchanged.
       
       At 30 WPM an element's key down lands exactly on a block boundary, the
       elements and the 8ms blocks being whole multiples of one another, and
       which block its deadline is reckoned from was settled by rounding.
       Reckoned from the edge as queued, it came out a block early; reckoned
       afresh, it comes out on the block the edge is really in, and the
       reading that decides the element -- which the KZD waits on -- is taken
       that much later, 6ms past the grid start. The KZD still carries the gap
       keyed, and the sidetone is where it was.
   
    simulations: Have squeeze_verdict apply the Dit Mem window
       A squeeze let go of in the leading Dit Mem % of a dah and its gap is
       owed no dit: send_iambic measures the release from when the dah was
       heard, and remembers the dit only past that window. squeeze_verdict
       checked iambic B's rule with no window at all, so at the application's
       33% it counted each of those as a failure -- 26 of the 125 at 30 WPM
       and 19 of the 94 at 40 -- when the keyer was doing what the setting
       asks.
       
       It now owes nothing for a release inside the window, allowing either
       answer on its edge as on a tone's start, and a fifth argument sets Dit
       Mem %. Every run passes at 33 as at 0. The kept sets are still run at
       0, where the window never applies, and come out unchanged.
   
    simulations: Bring the README up to the keyer's last two changes
       39926dd reckons a key down's deadline from the two clocks read afresh,
       and the 40 WPM squeeze released at 204ms -- a release landing on a
       tone's start, allowed either answer -- rounded the other way. Its run
       is regenerated and the table and the tie paragraph follow it. Every
       other stored run comes out byte identical.
       
       Say that nothing in the sweeps is ever late, so the take-back race
       39926dd removed could not show here, and that the harness runs with
       Dit Mem % at 0, which since 762c88b governs only a released squeeze.
   
    Version 2.0-rc27

    Remember any dit closed once a dah can be heard
       A dit closed during a dah was remembered only once it came more than
       dit_down_memory_delay after the dah was heard to start. The delay was
       0.05, commented as 5 ms, and nothing ever set it otherwise, so a dit
       closed in a dah's first 50ms was dropped unless it was held on or let
       go late enough for the Dit Mem window.
       
       The delay is gone, and its setter with it: a dit closed after the dah
       is heard is remembered. That takes in the tap the Dit Mem window used
       to judge, so what the setting still governs is a released squeeze, and
       its tooltip now says so.
   
    Queue a key down only once the element is wanted
       Every element after a burst's first had its sidetone key down queued at
       the start of the gap before it, before anything was known about it, and
       confirm_element took it back where the paddles wanted nothing. Taking
       back is a race with the output callback, and losing it sent a dit nobody
       had asked for, heard and on the air, a gap after the paddles had come
       off. The exclusive-mode logs show it at the end of burst after burst.
       
       The early queueing dates from when the element was settled at the start
       of the gap. It has been settled at its deadline since, and the key down
       need be in the queue no sooner than that. So confirm_element reads the
       paddles at the deadline and queues the key down only where they want an
       element. There is nothing to take back, and a lost race now costs a
       wanted key down a millisecond or two, carried in sidetone_lag for the
       catch-up to work off, rather than an extra element.
       
       sidetone_down_deadline reckons the deadline for an edge not yet queued,
       reading the two clocks itself. The dit send_burst supplied for an
       element nothing named is gone: nothing gets that far unasked. The dprint
       now says when a key down was queued past its deadline.
   
    Version 2.0-rc26

    Warn if inherent_sidetone_lag > .75 dit length

    Say when a queued key down could not be taken back
       confirm_element sends a dit for a key down the output callback has
       already placed, whether or not the paddles asked for one. Nothing in
       the log said so: the exclusive-mode logs show such dits after a burst
       has ended, and nothing to tell whether this thread read the paddles
       too late or the callback filled the block sooner than block_lead said.
       
       Print when the reading was taken against its deadline, where the edge
       was heard, and the block lead it was reckoned with.
   
