    Version 2.0-rc24
       Includes:
         - Skip detection for WASAPI block bursts (prevents lag runaway)
         - Reduced tolerable sidetone lag margin (1 ms, was 2 ms)
         - Reduced max catch-up fraction (3%, was 10%) for gentler trimming
   
    Only lower inherent_sidetone_lag, never raise it
       measure_inherent_sidetone_lag took the latest reading, which made the
       figure follow sidetone_lag wherever it went -- and the one direction
       sidetone_lag travels by itself is up: the correction in next_key_edge
       raises it and only the ease in a silence gives any of it back.
       
       That matters because of what the figure is for. The catch-up works off
       whatever the delay stands at above this plus a margin, so a reading
       taken fresh at every burst is the baseline chasing the very thing it is
       the baseline for: the delay is measured, blessed as inherent, and the
       trim then has nothing to work off. Lag that survived a silence was made
       permanent by being looked at.
       
       The premise for taking the latest was that every burst comes to the
       same figure to the frame, a burst's first key down being placed at a
       fixed delay. That holds for the first burst of a stream and no other:
       first_edge_lag returns None once a lag stands, so every burst after
       read whatever the ratchet had made of it.
       
       So the least of the readings now, never the latest, floored at what the
       device is asking for as things stand -- the new Audio.sidetone_delay_floor,
       over the existing wanted_sidetone_lag. Without the floor a minimum goes
       stale: the stream restarts on a device change and drops the audio's lag
       while this figure, being the keyer's, survives it, leaving the catch-up
       trimming toward a lag that is not there to be had. The floor cannot
       bring the runaway back, being read off the host's reported lead rather
       than off the lag the ratchet accumulated, and it is applied to each
       reading rather than kept, so a lead that widens for a moment lifts the
       figure only while it is wide.
       
       Driven over scripted per-burst readings: a device climbing 22.0 -> 47.8 ms
       now holds 22.0 throughout, where it used to follow all the way up; a
       device change from 14 to 31 ms is tracked up to the new floor; a
       one-burst wide lead lifts the figure and releases it. Squeeze sweeps
       pass at 20, 30, 40 and 50 WPM, all seven kept summaries are byte
       identical, and the host model is flat on both profiles.
       
       Also drops a stale comment above warned_unreactable describing the
       running average removed with update_sidetone_catchup.
   
    Simplify warn_if_unreactable to only check half-dit condition
       Changed warn_if_unreactable() to only warn when inherent_sidetone_lag
       is greater than half of dit_seconds. Removed all reactable_wpm() logic.
       
       The warning now has a single, simple condition: if the sidetone delay
       exceeds half the dit length, the paddles cannot be read before the
       element is heard, making keying to the sidetone impossible at that
       speed and WPM.
       
       The warning is suppressed once and only shown again if the speed or
       delay changes.
   
    Simplify sidetone catch-up fraction calculation
       Reduce sidetone catch-up fraction from 10% to 3%
       
       Changed from 'min(max_fraction, behind * per_second)' to
       'min(max_fraction, behind)'. The fraction now directly scales with
       how far behind the sidetone is, capped at max_sidetone_catchup_fraction.
       
       This removes the per_second rate parameter from the immediate fraction
       calculation, making the catch-up responsive to actual lag rather than
       a fixed rate. The adaptive rate adjustments still happen in
       update_sidetone_catchup for long-term tuning.
       
       Remove adaptive catch-up rate parameters
       
       Removed no-longer-needed variables now that sidetone_fraction is
       calculated directly as min(max_fraction, behind):
         - min_sidetone_catchup_per_second
         - max_sidetone_catchup_per_second
         - sidetone_catchup_per_second
         - sidetone_catchup_check_seconds
         - sidetone_catchup_change_seconds
         - sidetone_behind_average_weight
       
       Removed the update_sidetone_catchup() method which adaptively adjusted
       the rate based on whether catch-up was gaining or losing. With the
       direct fraction calculation, immediate responsiveness to lag is always
       maximized up to the max_fraction limit, making adaptive tuning redundant.
   
    Set tolerable sidetone lag margin to 1 millisecond

    Prevent lag runaway on Windows/WASAPI by detecting skipped blocks
       On WASAPI with shared mode (host period > block size), the host fills
       multiple blocks in one callback and outputBufferDacTime jumps a whole
       period at once. This causes edges queued for skipped blocks to be
       treated as delays the device is asking for, ratcheting sidetone_lag
       monotonically: 22 → 58 ms over 55 seconds at 32 WPM.
       
       A skipped block is not a delay requirement. Detect when dac_time has
       advanced more than one block period since the previous callback, and
       drag those edges without raising the lag. The lag now holds steady at
       22 ms on WASAPI, matching the behaviour on ALSA and single-block hosts.
       
       Model testing with three device profiles:
       - ALSA (real dac time): lag 28.5 ms, 1 ratchet ✓
       - steady lead (constant, no skips): lag 22.0 ms, 1 ratchet ✓
       - WASAPI (constant lead, skips): lag 22.0 ms, 1 ratchet ✓ (was 334 ms)
       
       The dragged edges cost a median 3 ms each, less than the trim that
       the runaway forced from every element downstream.
   
    Version 2.0-rc23

    simulations: Say that the sweeps never render a sample
       The second panel was described as what the operator heard, and it is
       the tone by itself -- not the mix it reaches them in. The output
       callback puts that mix together, with a queue of K4 packets, a buffer
       of what has been drawn but not yet played, a fade where that resumes
       after a silence and a crossfade under the tone's own edges, and none of
       it runs in these sets.
       
       Worth saying plainly, because 68b6ca4 is the case: a click at the end
       of every element, and the change that introduced it left all 370 plots
       identical. The edges say when a tone began and ended, which is what
       these sets are for. What it sounded like is not in them.
   
    audio: Drop the buffered K4 audio the tone is covering
       The output callback holds output_stream_leftover -- up to a packet of
       receive audio already drawn but not yet played -- across an element,
       and plays it out when the tone falls. That preserves nothing. The
       decoder caps its queue at two packets and drops the oldest, so the
       stream has moved on by the time the tone ends whatever the callback
       does with what it kept. What actually happened was that the stale audio
       played for a few milliseconds after the fall and then the waveform
       stepped to whatever the queue had, which is the click.
       
       The K4's own mute used to clear the leftover as a side effect -- its
       branch emptied data and wrote that back -- so this only came out when
       aa2ab90 took the mute away.
       
       Driving the real output callback over a steady sine as the receive
       audio, a single element, 20ms packets and 4ms blocks: 47 of the 61
       element lengths from 30 to 90ms stepped, and none does now. The step
       grew with the element, 0.18 at a 50ms dit against a signal of 0.5 and
       0.95 by 90ms -- most of the way across the waveform. It went for a
       straight key's own edges through key_sidetone as well as for the
       keyer's scheduled ones, which is the other half of what was reported.
       The 14 lengths that did not step are the ones where the leftover
       happened to run out in phase, which is why it came and went.
       
       Nothing takes from the queue while the tone is fully up, as before; the
       fall draws fresh audio and fades it in from nothing, which is what
       fade_in_k4 has always done where a block goes silent.
       
       All seven sweeps regenerate byte identical -- they drive next_key_edge
       rather than the output callback, so they say nothing about this either
       way -- and both squeeze sets still follow the rule in every run.
   
    simulations: Say that the sweeps do not exercise the lag ease
       The harness calls note_block_timing and next_key_edge itself rather
       than mix_sidetone, and the ease that gives back sidetone_lag lives in
       mix_sidetone. It would have nothing to do here in any case: the ease
       works only in the silence between characters, and a run is a single
       burst that settles its lag at the first key down and keeps it. Worth
       saying, since these sets coming out byte identical across that change
       is a fact about what they cover, not evidence the ease is harmless.
       
       And the tone-length paragraph called the lag one the keyer holds, which
       is no longer the whole of it.
   
    keyer: Give back sidetone_lag while the paddles are quiet
       The lag has been a one-way ratchet: an edge that lands late raises it
       and nothing lowers it short of a new stream. Four logs of real sending
       show that two ways, and both are the same thing.
       
       On the Linux machine the first paddle of the session closed while the
       app was still loading, at a block lead of 28.9ms against the 23 it
       settled at. The whole session was then monitored at 32.9ms for want of
       27 -- 220 elements without the figure moving once. On the Windows one
       the lead read a flat 18.0ms all session while the lag ground upward
       anyway, +0.2 to +3.7ms at a time, 22 to 47.8ms over 47 seconds and
       never back down.
       
       So a pause now eases the lag toward what the device is asking for --
       block lead plus a block, the same figure first_edge_lag places a
       burst's first key down at -- and never below it. Only while nothing is
       keyed, so the delay never moves during a character. This is not the
       idle drop that was taken out in fb865e8: that one threw the whole lag
       away and re-derived it from wherever in the block cycle the next paddle
       happened to fall, which is what made no two bursts alike. This keeps
       what was measured and lets go only of the part the device has stopped
       needing.
       
       A millisecond a second, chosen by replaying each log's own burst
       timings -- its leads, its silences and the ratchets it reveals --
       against candidate rates, and confirmed by driving the real
       ease_sidetone_lag over the same:
       
                               median delay      worst burst-to-burst
                               now    eased        now     eased
           linux 24 WPM        32.9   26.8         0.0       0.6
           linux 32 WPM        27.2   25.7         2.1       2.8
           win 24 WPM          37.4   35.9         3.7       5.2
           win 32 WPM          37.8   37.5         3.9       4.8
       
       Two milliseconds a second gains 0.6ms more on the machine with the
       stale strike and doubles the worst move on the two that grind. Where
       the grinding is a requirement that really is rising, the lag cannot get
       below it and this changes nothing at all: the edge drags and
       next_key_edge's ratchet puts it straight back.
       
       first_edge_lag keeps its meaning and loses the arithmetic to
       wanted_sidetone_lag, which both now ask.
       
       The sweeps stand in for mix_sidetone rather than calling it, so none of
       this runs in them; all seven regenerate byte identical, and both
       squeeze sets still follow the rule in every run. paddle_sweep.py binds
       the new method, first_edge_lag having stopped being self-contained.
   
    simulations: Say what the monitor mute does to the README's two claims
       The key panel is described as every KZD and KZU, and the summaries as
       what the K4 was sent. A burst mutes the K4's own sidetone monitor as it
       begins now, which the panel leaves out and the summaries keep, so both
       claims needed the qualification.
       
       And why a summary carries the mute without the command that undoes it:
       that one is sent from a timer, and the harness holds timers still along
       with the keyer's threads.
   
    simulations: Run again, and keep the monitor mute off the key panel
       paddle_sweep.py has not run since cd25c9d: Keyer.__init__ subscribes to
       KZL now, and the stub server it drives the keyer against had no
       subscribe_response to answer with. Stubbed, along with unsubscribe, and
       threading.Timer -- inert like the Thread beside it, since a real timer
       would fire on the wall clock in the middle of a run whose virtual one
       has not reached that instant.
       
       The key panel is documented as every KZD and KZU, and a burst now also
       sends ML0000 to mute the K4's sidetone monitor. Drawn with the rest it
       hung below the line looking like a key up, so the panel takes the key
       commands alone.
       
       The summaries record what was sent, ML0000 included, so those are
       regenerated. Every plot comes out byte identical to the ones committed,
       and so does every element, every edge and every KZD and KZU in the
       summaries: the only change in them is the line the monitor mute adds.
       Both squeeze sets still follow the rule in every run, 125/125 at 30 WPM
       and 94/94 at 40.
       
       Also drops a stub for saved_inherent_sidetone_lag, which the program
       has not had for some time.
   
    Mute the K4's sidetone audio, not all its audio
       While using the internal keyer, mute the K4's sidetone audio instead
       of muting all K4 audio. Also, when used near the K4, it removes the
       need to manually mute the sidetone when using the internal keyer.
   
    Version 2.0-rc22

    keyer: Decide an element at its grid start, not a gap before it
       send_burst asked the paddles twice about an element and the two are an
       inter-element gap apart: once as the gap began, and again at the
       element's own grid start, where confirm_element can still take back the
       key down it queued. The second reading settled what the element was.
       The first settled whether there was to be one at all -- and returning
       None there ended the burst on the spot.
       
       That is the wrong instant to be asking. The operator works to the
       sidetone, which is a whole audio delay behind, and through the whole of
       that gap the element before is still sounding to them. Coming off the
       dit and onto the dah while its tone runs is the ordinary way of sending
       a V, and it arrived after the keyer had declared the burst over: the
       dah began a burst of its own, the character went out broken in two, and
       the K4 was sent an inter-element space shorter than any it should ever
       be given -- 15ms, 12ms, 7ms in debug_rc21_linux-24WPM.txt, where twelve
       of eighty bursts were begun within two dits of the one before and five
       of those were begun while its tone was still sounding.
       
       So nothing is settled in the gap now. An element after a burst's first
       goes to start_element unasked for, and confirm_element's reading at the
       grid start decides both whether it happens and what it is, by the
       machinery that has always been there to take a tone back.
       
       A swept swap says what it buys, the dit let go while the third dit
       sounds and the dah closed a gap later, timed from where the operator
       hears that dit stop:
       
                           one burst up to   shortest space sent
           24 WPM before        -31.4 ms            4 ms
           24 WPM after         +16.6 ms           50 ms
           32 WPM before        -33.9 ms            1 ms
           32 WPM after          +2.1 ms           38 ms
       
       48ms wider at 24 WPM, 36ms at 32, and no space under a whole one is
       sent anywhere in the sweep. Replaying the session that opens that log
       gives ...- V where it gave ... S and then - T. Ultimatic comes through
       send_burst too and gains the same window.
       
       Not everything: a dah closed later than the grid start is still past
       saving, and four of that log's twelve were. Those want the delay itself
       smaller.
       
       All seven kept sweeps regenerate byte identical, plots and summaries
       alike, and both squeeze sets still follow the rule in every run --
       125/125 at 30 WPM and 94/94 at 40. They are silent on this change by
       construction: no paddle moves during the window it widens.
   
    keyer: Report a burst's first sidetone as the length it came out at
       end_element built end_sidetone_timestamp from the delay read back in
       start_element, which runs before this element's key down has been
       placed. At the first element of a session there is no placed edge to
       read a delay from at all, so what it got was a zero, and the tone was
       reported ending a whole lag before it really did: 17ms for a 50ms dit
       at 24 WPM, with the missing 33ms then showing up in the gap after it.
       One element per session, and every log opens with it.
       
       The delay is read in end_element now, where the key down has been
       waited for. Only the report was ever wrong -- both edges carry the same
       lag, and the callback's placed frames say the tone was its full 50.0ms
       -- but auto_space_until measures the gap since the last character from
       this, so with auto spacing on the first character of a session was
       judged against a gap a delay too long.
       
       element_sidetone_delay carried the stale reading and nothing else, so
       it goes, and plan_sidetone_catchup no longer returns what only it read.
       
       Seen in debug_rc21_linux-24WPM.txt and reproduced by replaying that
       session's paddle instants against the keyer over a virtual clock: the
       line now reads 99.5329 to 99.5829 against placed edges of 99.5329 and
       99.5829. All seven kept sweeps regenerate byte identical, plots and
       summaries alike, and both squeeze sets still follow the rule in every
