    Version 2.0-rc21

    keyer: Report the paddle and sidetone instants with debug on
       Three lines under Options.debug, for laying what the operator's hand did
       against what they heard:
       
           paddle dit_down at 100.1000
           paddle dah_down at 100.1000
           sidetone start at 100.1240
           sidetone end at 100.1640
           sidetone start at 100.2040
           sidetone end at 100.3240
       
       The paddle line covers a straight key's edges as well, both kinds
       landing at the same place in send_paddle_event. Its times are
       perf_counter for the paddles, which is the clock the sidetone lines are
       on too, so the two can be read across; a straight key's are its device's
       own clock and comparable only with themselves.
       
       The sidetone instants are the heard ones -- where the output callback
       really placed the edge, and where the up edge it was given will be
       heard -- rather than the keyed grid. They are said from the keyer's
       thread and not from where they are found: the callback that places an
       edge runs a couple of hundred times a second and is kept clear of
       anything that writes.
       
       Which means the lines come out in the order the keyer learns them and
       not in the order of the instants they carry. An element's end is
       reckoned when the element is decided, so it can be printed before a
       paddle edge that happened earlier. Sort on the timestamps rather than
       reading down the page.
       
       Every sweep comes out identical at both speeds, all 370 plots to the
       pixel and all seven summaries, and the whole behaviour suite passes.
   
    debug: Print keyed character

    Version 2.0-rc20

    keyer: Give a dit's key up whatever the element can spare
       The margin a dit's key up is queued with was one number trying to serve
       two ends. It is spent by giving up waiting for the tone that much
       sooner, so a large one protects the key up and costs the operator their
       answer, and a small one does the reverse. Ten milliseconds was wrong for
       both: too little to stop a dit running long when the keyer's thread was
       held up, and enough to put the last reading of the paddles before the
       tone at any speed worth the name.
       
       It is derived from the element now. The deadline is a block before the
       element ends and the element is heard a delay after it is keyed, so the
       reading falls on the tone exactly while the margin is no more than the
       element's rendered length less that delay -- and that is what is taken,
       all of it and no more. Where the element is shorter than the delay there
       is no margin to be had and no answer to be had either, which is what
       reactable_wpm reports and the keyer warns about.
       
       The two ends stop competing. The speed at which the last look still
       waits for the tone, over 24 block alignments a cell:
       
           delay     before     after
            16ms         38        79
            20ms         34        63
            24ms         30        52
            32ms         25        38
            48ms         19        25
       
       and at slow speeds the key up is given far more than ten milliseconds
       ever gave it, so the long dits are better covered as well.
       
       reactable_wpm loses the margin term with it, and agrees with the keyer
       again at every delay from 16 to 48 ms. The word a minute of room added
       to it for variability in the lag goes too, as asked.
       
       Four runs of the 40 WPM sweeps move with the reading: the look lands
       later now, so a tap at the boundary is caught by the element it landed
       in rather than the one after. dit-tap's boundary goes from 140ms to 148,
       swap's and tap's from 80 to 84, and no run loses its tapped element. The
       squeeze is unchanged at 125/125 and 94/94, the 30 WPM sets are identical
       to the pixel, and the whole behaviour suite passes.
   
