    Version 2.0-rc19

    Reduce sidetone_up_edge_margin_seconds 2 ms
       16 ms was too much and caused paddle-up events to be delayed, causing
       an extra dit to be sent.
   
    keyer: Warn where the speed outruns the sidetone delay
       Keying is a loop: the operator hears an element and answers it with the
       paddles. The keyer settles each element at the last look at the paddles,
       and for an answer to count that look has to come no earlier than the
       moment the element can be heard. It does not always wait that long -- a
       dit's key up is queued only once the element is known, and has to be in
       before the block carrying it is filled, so where the dit is short the
       look is taken early enough to leave room for it. Past that point the
       operator is sending open loop, and nothing says so.
       
       Something does now. The look waits for the tone exactly while
       
           whole milliseconds of a dit >= sidetone delay + up edge margin
       
       the dit being measured as end_element renders it, rounded up to the
       millisecond, and the block cancelling between the delay and the
       deadline's own rounding. reactable_wpm inverts that for the fastest
       speed a delay allows, and warn_if_unreactable says so when the speed is
       past it, naming the speed, the delay and the figure.
       
       Checked against the keyer rather than derived and left there. At each of
       ten delays from 8 to 48 ms, every speed from 12 to 60 WPM was run with
       the paddle swept across forty positions in the block cycle, and the
       highest speed at which the look never came early was compared with what
       reactable_wpm says:
       
           delay    says   really    delay    says   really
             8ms      52       52     28ms      27       27
            12ms      44       44     32ms      25       25
            16ms      38       38     36ms      23       23
            20ms      34       34     40ms      21       21
            24ms      30       30     48ms      19       19
       
       Two things that cost a word a minute each on the way to that. The dit
       the look is measured against is the rendered one, a whole millisecond
       and not 1.2/wpm. And twenty milliseconds plus sixteen is
       36.000000000000004 of them, which a ceiling reads as thirty-seven.
       
       Said when the speed changes and when a delay is first measured, and not
       again until one of them moves it -- an operator changing speed is asking
       the question, and the answer is worth having then rather than every
       element. It falls silent when the speed comes back within reach.
       
       paddle_sweep.py stubs the warning out. It drives the keyer with no
       window to show one in, and at 40 WPM against the 24 ms its harness
       settles at, the keyer now has something to say.
       
       Every sweep comes out identical at both speeds, all 370 plots to the
       pixel and all seven summaries, and the whole behaviour suite passes.
   
    audio: Report the block lead beside the sidetone delay
       A log said the stream opened at latency=12.00ms and then that the
       sidetone delay was 24 to 38, with nothing to connect the two. The
       reported latency is the depth PortAudio negotiated, printed once when
       the stream opens. What the delay is actually made of is the block lead
       -- outputBufferDacTime less currentTime, measured on every callback and
       kept at its furthest -- plus a block, which is where a burst's first key
       down is placed. Neither of those was reported anywhere, so a log could
       only be worked backwards from.
       
       Both are printed beside the delay now, on the line plan_sidetone_catchup
       already writes:
       
           inherent_sidetone_lag=0.024 sidetone_delay=0.024 block_lead=0.02 block=0.004
       
       where the sum is on the face of it.
       
       Read on the keyer's thread rather than printed where it is measured.
       note_block_timing runs on the output callback, a couple of hundred times
       a second, and that thread is kept clear of anything that writes --
       report_audio_glitches says as much about itself. Audio.block_timing just
       hands over what was last recorded.
       
       Every sweep comes out identical at both speeds, all 370 plots to the
       pixel and all seven summaries, and the whole behaviour suite passes.
   
    audio: Hold the sidetone lag across idle instead of dropping it
       Half a second of silence dropped sidetone_lag, so the next burst was
       rendered at the least delay the device allowed rather than inheriting
       the one before's. What that bought was the lowest delay each burst could
       have had. What it cost was that no two bursts had the same one: every
       burst re-derived the lag from whatever the block lead happened to be at
       the moment the paddle closed, and a debug log of real sending at 32 WPM
       came out at 24.3, 38.4, 33.7, 29.3 and on down to 26 ms -- character to
       character, on one machine, at one speed, where a dit is 37.5.
       
       The lag is established once now, by the first key down after the stream
       opens, and held. An edge asked for a whole lag ahead of the block
       carrying it is placed on its own frame, so a pause changes nothing.
       
       Over a host whose blocks are filled 20 to 23 ms ahead with a 12 ms spike
       two percent of the time, forty characters a session, the spread within
       a session:
       
                        before          after
           session 1    2.34 ms        0.02 ms
           session 2   12.02 ms        0.02 ms
           session 3   11.96 ms        0.02 ms
           ...
           session 8   11.97 ms        0.02 ms
       
       0.02 ms is one frame at 48 kHz.
       
       What is left is between sessions rather than within one: the figure is
       whatever the first key down after the stream opened called for, 26.9 to
       38.9 ms across those eight. It rises where the host later needs more,
       which is the drag correction in next_key_edge doing what it always did,
       and it returns to nothing only when a stream is opened.
       
       sidetone_lag_reset_seconds and quiet_frames went with it, neither having
       another reader.
       
       Every sweep comes out identical at both speeds, all 370 plots to the
       pixel and all seven summaries, and the whole behaviour suite passes.
   
