    panadapter: Tune by 10 kHz over the bare ground beside the waterfall
       The waterfall's widget is wider than the waterfall. The trace carries an
       axis at either edge, each insetting the span both displays show, and
       pyqtgraph holds the two X views together pixel for pixel -- so the
       waterfall's image stops short at both ends and leaves bare ground down
       either side, under the trace's scales.
       
       The arrow keys and the wheel nudged by a kilohertz over all of it, the
       ground included. Out there the cursor is past the last bin, so what is
       being reached for is off the edge of the span altogether, and a
       kilohertz a notch is a long way from bringing it into view. Over the
       ground the nudge is 10 kHz now. The waterfall proper is unchanged at
       1 kHz, and so are the mini-pan, the trace and the button area.
       
       The strips are measured from the trace's axes rather than from any
       figure of our own, so they stay under the scales whatever the ticks come
       out as, and they are as uneven as the axes are -- 40 px of S-units at one
       end against 56 of dB at the other, on the run below.
       
       The cursor is read rather than the event, because a key press carries no
       position of its own and the two have to agree about where the operator
       is.
       
       Driven over a real waterfall widget with the cursor stepped across it a
       pixel at a time, against a trace whose axes are 40 and 56 px wide: the
       ground answers 10 kHz from 0 to 39 and from 944 to 999, the waterfall
       1 kHz between, and the other three areas still answer 10, 100 and 1 Hz
       with None off all of them. An axis of no width leaves no strip at that
       end, which is the same statement.
   
    Version 2.0-rc18

    keyer: Don't catch up against a lag nobody has measured yet
       inherent_sidetone_lag is zero until an element has been sent to measure
       one over, and the first element of a session is planned before that has
       happened. Reckoned against zero, the whole of the audio's delay reads as
       something to work off, and the catch-up trims that element for it.
       
       Whether it did was a race. The first key down is queued by the paddle's
       own thread, and the delay this reads is only real once the output
       callback has placed it -- so it came and went with whether the callback
       got there before the keyer's thread did. The first dit of a session at
       30 WPM, which should be 40.00 ms:
       
           blocks filled     before    after
            10 ms ahead       40.00    40.00
            20 ms ahead       39.13    40.00
            30 ms ahead       40.00    40.00
            40 ms ahead       38.31    40.00
            60 ms ahead       37.52    40.00
       
       Nothing to measure against is nothing to catch up, and one element later
       there is a reading to use. This is the guard update_sidetone_catchup
       was given in a8b1842, applied to the figure it is given rather than only
       to the rate it sets.
       
       Every sweep comes out identical at both speeds, all 370 plots to the
       pixel and all seven summaries, and the whole behaviour suite passes.
   
