    audio: Place a burst's first key down at a fixed delay
       It was placed as early as the block would take it, and as early as
       possible is not the same distance twice. The edge is reported as the
       paddle closes, by which time the block carrying it is already being
       filled, so what it costs to reach the earliest free frame is however
       far into the block cycle the paddle happened to fall. That is where the
       whole spread of the monitoring delay comes from, and it is a block
       wide. Swept across one block period, on the 4 ms block the output
       buffer runs:
       
           blocks filled  6 ms ahead     6.25 to 10.00 ms
                         10 ms ahead    10.00 to 13.75 ms
                         16 ms ahead    16.25 to 20.00 ms
                         24 ms ahead    24.00 to 27.75 ms
       
       Placed a block past the lead instead, each of those comes out at 10.00,
       14.00, 20.00 and 28.00 every time, to the frame. A host that fills
       blocks in bursts, where the lead itself wanders from 22 to 32 ms, goes
       from a 7.30 ms spread to 0.02.
       
       The trade is the best case and not the worst. The burst is never
       monitored sooner than this and never later either, and a constant delay
       is not heard where one that moves from character to character is. It is
       worth the couple of milliseconds to inherent_sidetone_lag as well, which
       until now had a block of arbitrariness to hold the least of.
       
       Only a burst's first key down. A lag already standing says a burst is
       under way, and an edge arriving late inside one is a stall for the
       keyer's re-anchor and catch-up to work off rather than a fresh delay to
       settle on.
       
       The lead kept is the furthest a block has lately been filled, so the
       target can land past the block in hand; the edge then goes back in the
       queue for the block that does carry it, and the lag being already set,
       it is placed exactly there. Under a bursty host that is every burst, and
       the element counts are unchanged.
       
       Every sweep comes out identical at both speeds, the squeeze 125/125 and
       94/94, and the whole behaviour suite passes. paddle_sweep.py binds the
       new method on the Audio stand-in it drives.
   
    keyer: Measure a tap from the last look at the paddles
       An element is settled by a reading of the paddles taken once its tone
       can be heard, and the memory of the element after it measures a tap
       from that element's heard start. Those were the same instant until the
       key up was given a margin to be queued in: a dit's key up has only what
       is left of the dit, so the look is now taken as much as
       sidetone_up_edge_margin_seconds before the tone is audible.
       
       That left a window between the look and the heard start that nothing
       covered -- too late for the reading that settled the element it landed
       in, too early for the next element's memory. A tap falling entirely
       inside it was not deferred or misplaced but lost: the operator tapped a
       paddle and no element came out. At 40 WPM the dah-held sweep lost the
       dit at 140ms and 144ms and the dit-held sweep lost the dah at 80ms; at
       45 WPM, 128ms and 76ms.
       
       So a tap is measured from the last look instead, and the two windows
       meet. A squeeze is still measured from the heard start, which is what
       the operator lets go by, and a burst's first element still measures
       both from its keyed start: the look that settled it consulted no memory,
       having no element before it to remember against, so a tap made between
       the paddle closing and that look has only the next element's memory to
       be caught by.
       
       No tap is lost at 40 or 45 WPM in any of the four scenarios now. Every
       sweep comes out identical at both kept speeds, the squeeze 125/125 and
       94/94, and the whole behaviour suite passes.
       
       previous_elt_ts went with it. send_burst passed it to both
       choose_elements and neither had read it since the memories were moved
       onto the heard start; the slot now carries the look.
   
    keying_simulations: Add the squeeze at 40 WPM
       Nothing was watching above 30 WPM, and the speed is not incidental to
       this keyer. Two of the things these sets hold in place are measured
       against an element's length, and a dit at 40 WPM is a quarter shorter
       than at 30. A dit's key up is the tightest edge in the scheme -- it is
       queued only once the tone can be heard, so what is left of the dit
       after the audio's delay is all the notice it gets -- and it was that
       margin, invisible at 30 WPM, that sidetone_up_edge_margin_seconds was
       added for.
       
       Ninety-four runs, 4ms to 376ms, all following the rule:
       
             4-80ms      the first dit     dit dah
            84-200ms     the first dah     dit dah dit
           204-264ms     the second dit    dit dah dit dah
           268-376ms     the second dah    dit dah dit dah dit
       
       paddle_sweep.py takes the speed as an optional fourth argument. At
       anything but 30 the swept moment and the length of the run scale with
       it, so a sweep covers the same ground rather than the same
       milliseconds: the ranges are cut to elements, not to time. The step
       stays at 4ms and the ends are moved to whole steps of it, so a run
       still lands where the elements do. At 30 WPM nothing moves -- all three
       sets come out byte identical, and none of their 202 plots is touched.
       
       The summaries now carry the speed in their names, so the two squeezes
       do not collide.
       
       Three of the tones at this speed start on a step of the sweep, so three
       runs let the squeeze go at the very instant a tone begins. They do not
       all fall the same way here -- 84 and 204 count as during the tone
       beginning, 264 as during the one ending -- which is the rounding
       squeeze_verdict was taught to allow either way in 38cd76a. Before that
       the release at 204ms was reported as a failure and was not one.
   
    keying_simulations: Let a release landing on a tone's start be either
       At 40 WPM the squeeze sweep called the release at 204ms wrong. The
       keyer had done nothing wrong. 204ms is the third tone's start exactly:
       the step is a whole number of milliseconds and so are the elements, so
       at some speeds a run puts the release on a tone's start to the last bit.
       
       The rule has nothing to say about that instant. The space of the window
       before has just run out and the tone of the window after has not yet
       sounded for any length of time at all, and a squeeze let go of there
       can be said to owe either. Which side a run comes down on is settled by
       which way a subtraction rounded -- separately here and in the keyer,
       which reach the same quantity by different routes, a stream clock and a
       paddle clock apart, and so need not agree. At 30 WPM they happened to;
       at 40 they did not.
       
       So a release within a microsecond of a tone's start is allowed either
       answer. That is a thousand times finer than anything the keying turns
       on and fifty times finer than the frame an edge is placed on, and a
       hundred million times the rounding it exists to absorb. Nothing really
       wrong is wrong by a microsecond: a squeeze that earns the wrong number
       of elements earns a whole one too many or too few.
       
       The squeeze now passes at 20, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45 and 50 WPM, every run
       of every one. All three committed summaries come out identical, and the
       check is no blunter for it -- restoring the 6c715bb guard still takes
       the set from 125 to 25, and measuring a burst's first element like the
       rest still loses the six releases inside the lag.
       
       Two numbers in the README move with it, the three runs on a boundary no
       longer counting as failures: measuring the memories from the keyed
       start loses 21 rather than 24, and estimating the heard instant now
       loses nothing at all, where it lost the single release at 344ms. That
       last one leaves the set saying nothing either way about taking the
       placed edge over the estimate, and the README says so.
   
    keyer: Give an element's key up a margin it can live on
       A key up is queued only once the tone can be heard, the paddles being
       read as late as they can be, so what is left of the element after the
       audio's delay is all the notice it gets. Every other edge has a whole
       element or gap. A dit is the shortest element there is, so a dit's key
       up is the tightest edge in the scheme, and how tight depends on how far
       ahead the device fills its blocks -- at 40 WPM, the slack it was queued
       with:
       
           blocks filled  8 ms ahead    16.17 ms
                         16 ms ahead     8.00 ms
                         24 ms ahead     2.00 ms
                         32 ms ahead     2.00 ms
       
       Two milliseconds is no margin for a thread sharing a process with the
       panadapter. Missing the block does not move the sending any more -- the
       lag and the delay reading are both left alone now -- so the whole cost
       falls on that one tone's length, and a dit sounded as much as half as
       long again as it was meant to.
       
       So the key up gets a margin of its own, spent by giving up waiting for
       the tone that much sooner. It holds the slack at 16 ms at every lead
       above. Over twelve seeds with the keyer's thread woken up to N ms late,
       the share of dits sounding more than a millisecond long, and the worst
       one:
       
                            before          after
           35 WPM, 16 ms   9.4%  3.44    0.0%  1.00
           35 WPM, 24 ms  30.6% 12.37   24.0%  8.69
           40 WPM, 12 ms  17.6%  2.00    0.0%  0.00
           40 WPM, 16 ms  30.1%  8.94    0.0%  0.00
           40 WPM, 20 ms  38.6% 12.46   14.2%  7.87
           40 WPM, 24 ms  42.9% 16.17   16.2%  7.90
       
       What it costs is the last reading of the paddles being that much
       earlier, and only where the element had less than the margin to spare.
       At 30 WPM and below that is nowhere: every run of all four sweeps comes
       out identical at 25 and at 30 WPM, the squeeze 150/150 and 125/125. Run
       at speeds the sweeps are not kept at, the memory boundaries move a step:
       1 run of 84 at 35 WPM, 4 of 116 at 40, 7 of 66 at 45. The squeeze
       verdict is unchanged at every speed tried.
   
    keyer: Start the behind average from a reading, not from zero
       The catch-up rate is moved by whether the running average of what is
       left to work off has risen or fallen over the last second. The average
       began at zero, so its climb out of that zero was there to be read as a
       rise, and the check read it as one: with a lag steadily coming in, the
       rate doubled twice over the first two seconds and the elements it
       doubled were shortened twice as hard as they needed to be.
       
       Two things were needed to stop it. The first reading now becomes the
       average outright rather than a tenth of the way from a zero nobody
       measured. And nothing is fed in at all until the inherent lag has been
       measured, because until then the first half of the fix buys nothing:
       behind is reckoned against that lag, and the first element of a session
       is planned before any edge has been placed, so what it offers is the
       whole of a delay that is itself still zero.
       
       At 30 WPM with a 15 ms lag standing, the rate the elements were trimmed
       at, against the rate the lag was actually coming in at:
       
                        before        after
            1.0 s        1.96          1.12
            1.5 s        2.16          0.86
            2.0 s        1.52          1.21
       
       It costs nothing to get right. Over the same burst the mean and worst
       error of the heard interval are identical either way, at a 5, 10, 15 and
       25 ms standing lag alike -- the gentler pull simply takes a few more
       elements, which is what it is for. All four keying sweeps come out
       identical, the squeeze still 125/125.
   
    keyer: Don't read a dragged key up as the audio's delay changing
       sidetone_edge_shift is how far the output callback displaced an edge
       from the time it was asked for, and plan_sidetone_catchup reckons the
       audio's delay from it. For an edge placed on its own frame that is
       exactly sidetone_lag, which is what it wants.
       
       An edge the block had no room left for is displaced by the lag plus
       however late the keyer was with it, and since a key up no longer puts
       its shortfall into the lag, only the first of those is the audio's
       doing. Taken as a delay, the rest is a change that never happened: the
       schedule is moved by the whole of it at the next element and moved back
       at the one after, a wobble with nothing behind it. It drove
       sidetone_delay_used negative as well -- as far as -8.6 ms in one probe,
       the schedule held behind the grid, making the monitoring delay worse
       than the audio ever asked for.
       
       So such an edge leaves the reading standing, the lag not having moved.
       
       Over twelve seeds of a three second squeeze with the keyer's thread
       woken up to N ms late -- the mean error of the heard interval, and the
       share of elements more than 2 ms off the grid:
       
                               before            after
           35 WPM, 20 ms    0.82 ms   7.5%    0.68 ms   3.6%
           35 WPM, 24 ms    1.92 ms  25.4%    1.33 ms  13.7%
           40 WPM, 16 ms    0.71 ms  10.8%    0.14 ms   3.2%
           40 WPM, 20 ms    1.82 ms  27.7%    0.76 ms  12.8%
           40 WPM, 24 ms    3.18 ms  36.6%    2.05 ms  23.7%
       
       All four keying sweeps come out identical, the squeeze still 125/125.
   
    keyer: Keep the schedule's offset across a re-anchor
       A stall longer than an inter-element gap leaves the grid start this
       element should have been keyed on in the past, and the grid is moved up
       so that the elements after it are not all keyed late. The sidetone edge
       goes in sidetone_delay_used ahead of the grid, so moving the grid to now
       would put the edge that far behind it, which is why the offset was being
       dropped at the same time.
       
       Move the grid to now plus the offset instead. The edge is what must not
       be asked for in the past, and it then lands at now exactly as before,
       with the offset kept.
       
       Nothing about this is audible either way, and the measurements say so:
       the grid and the offset move together and cancel, so the edge is at the
       same instant under both, and so is every edge after it. Over twelve
       seeds of a three second squeeze with the keyer's thread woken up to N ms
       late, the mean error of the heard interval:
       
                             before     after
           40 WPM, 16 ms    0.71 ms   0.71 ms
           40 WPM, 20 ms    1.72 ms   1.82 ms
           40 WPM, 24 ms    2.96 ms   3.18 ms
       
       What it is for is saying the right thing. The offset is the catch-up's
       record of how much of the audio's lag it has already worked off, and a
       stall is no reason to claim it never did. The old comment's "giving it
       up costs nothing at once, and the catch-up works it back over the
       elements after" was describing work that need not be done at all.
       
       All four keying sweeps come out identical, the squeeze still 125/125.
   
    keying_simulations: Regenerate the sets and correct the README
       The plots were last made before the sidetone catch-up stopped running
       over these runs. Every tone now comes out at exactly the length its KZU
       carries, where before the catch-up was trimming them a fraction -- 119ms
       against 0120, 39ms against 0040 -- so all 202 plots are redrawn.
       
       Two squeeze bands moved by one step, to 108-264 and 268-344. The third
       and fourth tones become audible at exactly 264ms and 344ms, the sweep's
       4ms step lands on both, and those two runs are the squeeze being let go
       of at the very instant a tone begins. They now fall into the band
       ending rather than the one starting, which is where the release at
       104ms already fell: all three coincidences go the same way now.
       
       The numbers the squeeze section quotes are re-measured against the
       current source rather than carried forward:
       
         - memories from the keyed start: 24 of 125 wrong, not 22, and two of
           the four runs of failures reach one step further
         - a burst's first element measured like the rest: 6 wrong, 4 to 24ms,
           unchanged
         - the 6c715bb guard restored: 125 to 25, unchanged, and the swap,
           tap and dit-tap sweeps still byte identical under every one of them
       
       The estimate the keyer falls back on when an edge is never placed is
       now reckoned from the delay the last placed edge was given rather than
       from a saved inherent lag, and it comes to the same instant as the
       placed edge here: using it throughout now costs one run, not the last
       release of every band. Said so, rather than leaving the old claim
       standing.
       
       Dropped the paragraph about carrying inherent_sidetone_lag on
       previous_elt_ts. Neither spelling is in the keyer any more.
   
    keyer: Only a key down carries a late edge into the lag
       Every sidetone edge but a burst's first key down is queued ahead of the
       block that carries it, but not all with the same notice. A key up goes
       in only once the tone can be heard, so a dit's has what is left of the
       dit after the audio's delay, less the lead the block is filled with:
       40 ms at 20 WPM, 16 at 30, and at 40 a single block. A dah's has three
       times the element.
       
       Missing that block put the shortfall into sidetone_lag, which shifts
       every later edge in the burst: the dit came out long and everything
       after it moved, which plan_sidetone_catchup then read as the audio
       having fallen behind and worked off by trimming the elements that
       followed. One slip became several elements of wandering, and it could
       only ever start on a dit -- which is where the unevenness at dit to dah
       and dah to dit came from.
       
       The lag is for an edge late by definition. A key up now takes the
       earliest frame the block has left and nothing more, costing the gap
       after it the millisecond or two the tone overran.
       
       Over a three second squeeze with the keyer's thread woken up to N ms
       late, the spread of the heard interval at 40 WPM:
       
                    12 ms   16 ms   20 ms   24 ms
           before    4.00   23.85   33.38   87.69
           after     4.00   12.12   19.27   18.79
       
       Nothing changes where the thread is on time: all four keying sweeps
       come out identical to before, the squeeze still 125/125.
   
    Wait for sidetone using the actual sidetone delay
       Instead of using a saved initial delay value, use
       the current sidetone delay value when waiting for
       the sidetone to start.
   
    Add 5 ms to the tolerable sidetone lag
       The tolerable sidetone is based on inherent_sidetone_lag
   
    No longer save and restore inherent_sidetone_lag

    Version 2.0-rc17

    keyer: Measure a squeeze release from what was audible
       A squeeze let go of owes one more element -- the opposite of the one it
       was let go of during -- and the rule is about what the operator can
       hear. The memories were measuring from the element as keyed, which is a
       lag earlier, so a release landing after the next element had been keyed
       but before any of it was audible counted as falling during that element
       and put an unasked-for one after it.
       
       They measure from the heard start now, and from where the output
       callback really placed the edge rather than the keyed start plus
       inherent_sidetone_lag: an edge that lands late is further off than the
       inherent lag admits, 24ms rather than 16 for the second element of a
       burst.
       
       A burst's first element keeps its keyed start. That one the operator
       began themselves by closing the paddle, and it is the only element
       whose beginning is known to them without hearing it, so a squeeze let
       go of before it can be heard has still been let go of during it.
       
       previous_elt_ts is that keyed start now, taken from start_element.
       send_burst read the clock at the top of its loop, a whole inter-element
       gap earlier for every element but a burst's first, and the lag had been
       papering over the difference.
       
       The squeeze sweep was judging the wrong timeline too -- it cut its
       windows from the keyed grid and declined to judge the twenty releases
       falling in a gap. It cuts them from the sidetone now, a tone plus the
       space after it, so every release falls in exactly one window and all of
       them are judged. It also runs to 500ms rather than 240, two whole
       cycles of the squeeze rather than one, so the bands are seen to repeat
       rather than assumed to.
       
       All 125 follow the rule. The swap, tap and dit-tap sweeps are byte
       identical and notice none of this: measuring the squeeze from the keyed
       start costs 22 of the 125, estimating the heard start costs 5,
       measuring a burst's first element like the rest costs 6, and 6c715bb's
       guard restored takes it from 125 to 25.
   
    Version 2.0-rc16

    keying_simulations: Add the released-squeeze sweep
       Sixty runs at 30 WPM: both paddles closed at time zero and both let go
       together at a moment moving 4ms per run from 4ms to 240ms -- one whole
       dit-gap-dah-gap cycle of the squeeze being released, so the release
       falls in every part of it.
       
       This is the case iambic B exists for, and alone among the sets here it
       is checked as well as drawn: each run says what was in progress, what
       was owed for it, and what followed, and the run ends with a count.
       Forty releases land during an element and thirty-six get their element.
       
       The four that do not are the releases at 4 to 16ms, inside
       inherent_sidetone_lag, so the release lands no later than the moment
       the element is reckoned to have been heard. They behave the same before
       6c715bb and after efd5c5c. The twenty releases landing in a gap between
       elements are drawn but not judged: what is owed there is a question
       this sweep cannot settle.
       
       The set is here because 6c715bb broke exactly this and no sweep
       noticed. Restoring that guard today leaves the swap, tap and dit-tap
       sweeps byte identical and takes this one from 36 to 0.
   
    keyer: Let a released squeeze have its element again
       6c715bb required a remembered paddle to have been closed during the
       element it is remembered from. A squeeze released is the opposite of
       that -- both paddles closed from before the element began and let go
       part way through it -- so the extra element iambic B owes was never
       sent. Swept across the element at 5ms steps, 29 of 31 releases produced
       it before that commit and none after.
       
       Three cases have to be told apart, and closed-during-the-element only
       separates the first:
       
                                   closed during?  other still held?  wanted
           dah tapped mid element       yes              yes          insert
           swap from dit to dah         no               yes          suppress
           squeeze released             no               no           insert
       
       So a released paddle earns its element where it was closed during that
       element, or where the other paddle has been let go of as well. A paddle
       released while the other is still held stays suppressed, which is what
       6c715bb was for.
       
       The live paddle states are kept apart from the memories now: a memory
       asks what the other paddle is really doing and must not be answered
       with another memory.
       
       The swap, dah-tap and dit-tap sweeps all come out byte identical. None
       of them covered a released squeeze, which is how this got through.
   
    keying_simulations: Add the dah-held, dit-tapped sweep
       Fifty-six runs at 30 WPM: the dah paddle closed at time zero and held,
       the dit paddle tapped for 5ms at a moment moving 4ms per run from 80ms
       to 300ms -- nearly five dah element periods, a dah being three times a
       dit.
       
       Every run inserts exactly one dit into the stream of dahs, never two
       and never none, and the dahs resume after it. Only which element the
       dit becomes varies:
       
            80-184ms   dah dit dah dah dah dah
           188-300ms   dah dah dit dah dah dah
       
       The boundary sits at 188ms rather than the 160ms where the second dah
       begins. Those 28ms are send_burst asking the paddles a second time: an
       element is started before it is known what it is and read again once
       its sidetone can be heard, so a tap arriving up to 184ms still catches
       the element that began at 160ms.
       
       paddle_sweep.py gains this as a third scenario rather than a script of
       its own, with the held paddle, the hold length and the tap range now
       per scenario. It reproduces the swap and tap sweeps unchanged. Its
       summary is named for the scenario, so a second set written beside the
       first no longer overwrites it; the existing summary.json is renamed to
       match.
   
    keyer: Time an auto space from the element's keyed end
       start_element measures the gap the K4 is told from
       end_element_timestamp, so a hold reckoned from end_sidetone_timestamp
       carried the difference between the two -- the delay the audio adds --
       into every auto space. Measured with a 16ms delay, a character space
       went out as 135ms where it should be 120, and a word space as 296
       where it should be 280.
       
       The threshold still measures from the heard end: whether a space was
       meant is a question about what the operator heard, while what is
       returned is an instant to key at. A gap too short to be a space is
       still not held out, and with auto spacing off the operator's own gap
       still stands.
   
    keyer: Read the paddles once in send_ultimatic's choose_element
       The four timestamps were read afresh at every comparison, and the down
       pair read a second time for the tie-break, so a comparison could pair
       one paddle as it was against another as it had become. The thread the
       paddles' device reports on writes them, and since release_paddles so
       does whichever thread lets that device go.
       
       Read once into locals, as send_iambic already reads them. The sweeps
       come out identical either way, both scenarios and all 42 runs.
   
    keyer: Send the KZU for a key whose device is taken away
       Silencing the sidetone was not the half of it. What KZD starts only KZU
       ends, and the release that would have sent it is exactly what is never
       coming when the keyer is closed or its device changed with the key
       down. The K4 was left keyed -- transmitting, and with nothing to be
       heard, the tone having just been stopped.
       
       A Vail Adapter meets this oftener than any other device: its own keyer
       sends the elements and reports each of them as a key down and a key up,
       so a change arriving mid element finds the key down.
       
       release_paddles now ends the key on the air as well, measuring the key
       down in real time -- the device's own clock stops with the device.
       Whether a KZD is outstanding is kept in k4_keyed, the timestamps being
       unable to say: a session's first key down is reported at the very zero
       they start from. send_straight_key sends its own KZU only while the K4
       is still keyed, so a device given back while the key is held -- the
       settings page rebuilt under it -- does not key it up twice.
   
    keyer: Let the paddles go when their device is taken away
       Changing the device type cancels the callback the paddles report
       through but leaves the keyer running. The internal keyer reads the
       paddles afresh at every element and sends for as long as one of them
       reads closed, so a paddle held across the change read closed for ever:
       elements, and the KZD and KZU for each of them, going out for as long
       as the app ran. Straight keying has no such loop and showed the same
       thing as a tone left sounding.
       
       Whatever the device last reported closed is taken as open once it has
       been let go of, and any tone it started that nothing else would end is
       ended. The element under way still goes out as it was keyed and the
       burst ends at the boundary after it, rather than the thread being
       stopped mid element.
       
       stop_keying becomes release_paddles, which close() asks for with
       closing=True: a keyer going away with its device keys nothing further,
       where one that stays keys on for whatever device comes next.
   
    Version 2.0-rc15

    keyer: Stop keying the paddles once the keyer has closed
       Leaving CW mode closes the keyer, but the paddles' devices were left
       calling it. Since a burst's first key down and a straight key's two
       edges are keyed on the device's own thread, the key down still went in
       while the key up belonged to a keyer thread that was no longer there:
       a paddle touched in a non-CW mode was heard as a tone that never
       stopped.
       
       The devices are released on close, after the keyer thread is joined and
       not before -- a burst runs until the paddle is released, and that
       release comes through the same callback, so cancelling it first left the
       join waiting for ever. A closed flag covers the callback already in
       flight, which cancelling cannot reach into, and stop_keying ends
       whatever tone was started that the keyer thread will not.
       
       MidiInput.cancel_message_callback forgets the callback as SerialInput's
       does: poll_new_device reinstalls what it keeps, so a cancelled one came
       back the next time the device changed.
   
    gui: Keep a group box's tight stylesheet off the boxes inside it
       dcba440 left the settings boxes on the Server tab with their titles
       painted over by their own contents. The fault was not in _titled_grid,
       which builds them, but in what reaches them: a stylesheet set on a
       widget applies to its descendants as well, and the tab's own group --
       written label: '' in the config -- carries an unqualified
       
           QGroupBox { margin-top: ...px }
       
       which therefore styles every group box nested anywhere inside it.
       
       Before that commit the outer box reserved a whole line of title height
       and the children inherited a margin that happened to be about right for
       theirs. dcba440 correctly dropped it to 2px for a box with no title of
       its own, and the children inherited that instead, leaving their titles
       nowhere to go. The leak was always there; only the value changed, from
       one that worked by accident to one that does not.
       
       So the selector is qualified with the box's own object name.
       qt_tight_groupbox_stylesheet takes one, and qt_tighten_groupbox sets
       the name, builds the sheet and sizes the title -- which also folds
       together the three steps qt_group_box and label_and_leading_frame were
       each doing by hand.
       
       _titled_grid was the visible casualty, not the only one. Seven group
       boxes are built without a stylesheet of their own and were relying on
       whatever an ancestor happened to say: _titled_grid's, remote_settings',
       the CW tab's dropdown and memory percent frames, the MIDI and serial
       Device boxes, and the menu's. Each now tightens itself, so it carries a
       margin measured for its own title rather than falling back to Qt's
       roomier native rendering.
       
       The titles are back at the 50px boxes they were before dcba440 while
       the outer box keeps that commit's 16px saving -- the block measures
       218px against 234 before it. What dcba440 was for is untouched: the
       Server tab's rows are still 26px, the top of that tab still 2px, and
       RIT and XIT still land together at 163 with the controls area 188 tall.
   
    Reduce the sidetone fade-out to 4 ms
       Now it matches the fade-in time. They should be equal.
   
    keyer: Remember a paddle only where it was closed during the element
       Once the operator has swapped from the dit paddle to the dah paddle,
       nothing should follow the first dah but dahs. Thirteen runs of a
       twenty-one run sweep put a dit there instead -- as element 3 where the
       swap came before 108ms, and as element 4 from 140ms up.
       
       The iambic B dit memory was firing on the release alone:
       
           dit_up_ts - previous_elt_ts > dit_up_memory_delay
       
       with the delay percentage defaulting to zero, so any release after the
       dah began satisfied it. Tracing the decisions showed dit_down sitting
       well before that dah started in every one of those runs. What the
       memory took for a tap was the operator coming off the dit paddle as the
       dah took over, and it answered by sending one more of the element they
       had just stopped asking for.
       
       So the memory now wants the paddle closed during the element it is
       remembered from, not merely released during it. A tap -- closed and
       released inside one element, too short to be seen when the next is
       chosen -- is what it is for and still fires. A paddle closed during the
       element and still held never needed it: dit and dah already have that.
       
       The same guard goes on the dah memory, which had the fault mirrored.
       
       Swept at 30 WPM: no dit follows the first dah in any of the 21 runs,
       against 13 before, and the only thing that still varies is how many
       dits precede the swap. The dah tap sweep is the regression test for the
       memory itself, a tap being exactly what it exists for, and it comes out
       untouched -- all 21 runs, every element and every KZD and KZU identical.
       
       keying_simulations holds that swap sweep, its summary, the harness that
       produced it and a README. The harness drives the real Keyer over a
       virtual clock: paddle events go in through send_paddle_event on the
       thread a paddle's device reports on, send_iambic runs on the keyer's
       thread a hop later, and the sidetone plotted is the frames
       next_key_edge actually placed.
   
    keyer: Carry the sidetone lag on the element's side of the memories
       Only the iambic memories want the lag at all. They measure a paddle
       against when the previous element was started, and the two are on
       different clocks: the paddle timestamps are real time, when the
       operator's hand moved, while previous_elt_ts is when the keyer began
       the element, which is not heard until a lag later -- and it is what is
       heard that the operator works the paddles to.
       
       Taking the lag off all four paddle timestamps put both sides on that
       timebase, but by moving the side that has three other readers. The
       comparisons of one paddle against another take no notice of it either
       way, a shift applied to all four cancelling between them, so it belongs
       where only one side of the comparison moves.
       
       The two spellings are the same arithmetic -- (paddle - lag) - elt_ts
       against paddle - (elt_ts + lag) -- and the sweep says so: 21 runs of a
       dit-to-dah swap at 30 WPM come out identical, every element and every
       KZD and KZU alike.
       
       The sign is not free, though, and is worth recording here. Adding the
       lag to the paddle timestamps rather than subtracting it lands two lags
       from where it should be, and every one of those 21 runs then carries an
       unasked-for dit where eight of them did not.
   
