       combobox against 3px over before. The text still has at least 2px to
       spare at every size, and button widths are unchanged.
       
       qt_button_stylesheet is shared, so this is every button, not only the
       Main tab's. The same pairing occurs elsewhere -- the Receive tab's NR
       and NB groups among them -- and singling out one tab's toggle buttons
       would need a per-widget option and leave the app inconsistent with
       itself.
   
    keyer: Queue a burst's first key down on the paddle's own thread
       A burst's first element is the one edge in a burst that cannot be
       scheduled ahead: every element after it is queued a gap before it is
       due and lands on its own frame, while this one is asked for the moment
       it is keyed and dragged to the earliest frame the output callback has
       left. What that drag comes to is sidetone_lag, which shifts every later
       edge in the burst and is what the catch-up then spends the burst
       working off, so queuing it sooner is worth the whole burst's delay and
       not one edge's, and it lowers the inherent lag measured from it too.
       
       start_burst_sidetone queues it when the paddle closes, on the thread
       the paddle's device reports on, under three conditions. Only between
       bursts, since a paddle closing inside one is the keyer's own to
       schedule and a second closing before the keyer has taken the first
       would queue a second key down against the one key up that is coming.
       Not where auto spacing is to hold the element off, which is settled
       there against the instant the paddle closed and left alone afterwards.
       And under a lock, so that a burst starting in that window cannot queue
       an edge of its own as well.
       
       start_element anchors the grid on that instant rather than on its own
       clock: the tone and the grid have to be struck from the same one, or
       the element is rendered longer than it was keyed by the whole hop. The
       first element's KZD gap is measured from there too, so it carries the
       gap the operator left and not that gap plus the wake. Where nothing is
       wanted by the time the keyer looks, placed_first_element takes the edge
       back; where it cannot, the element goes out whole, the rule
       confirm_element already follows for the elements after a first.
       
       wait_for_auto_space splits into auto_space_until(now) so both threads
       can ask it of an instant rather than of the clock.
       
       Simulated over 336 runs a version -- two host buffer shapes, three
       thread hops, eight block phases and seven paddle scripts: the first
       tone no longer moves with the hop in any of 64 configurations, where it
       did in 32, and its spread comes in from 17-28ms to 17-24ms on a regular
       host and from 20-36ms to 20-31ms on WASAPI shared. No tone is left
       unended either side. Bursts also stop dropping an element to a late
       wake, and the auto spaced word gap holds at 353-360ms where it stretched
       to 384ms.
       
       Three further changes of behaviour, all of them intended: a contact too
       brief for the keyer to see sends an element where its edge cannot be
       taken back, rather than being dropped silently; bursts keep elements
       they used to lose; and the catch-up, now sometimes seeing the burst's
       real lag at the first element instead of the second, trims that element
       by up to .19ms on a dit and 1.5ms on a dah.
   
    keyer: Key a straight key's sidetone on the device's own thread
       A straight key's edge cannot be scheduled ahead the way the keyer's
       elements are, so every step between the contact and the queued edge is
       monitoring delay. The largest of them was waking the keyer's thread
       through paddle_event_queue: measured under a load like the app's own,
       .15ms at the median and a millisecond or two at the worst, and up to a
       five millisecond switch interval where something holds the GIL without
       yielding. The spread is as audible as the delay.
       
       send_paddle_event now keys the sidetone itself, ahead of its own
       bookkeeping and the queue. Both edges, since what is heard as the
       element's length is the difference between them: an edge left to the
       keyer's thread would put the whole hop into every element.
       send_straight_key keeps the K4's half -- the KZD and KZU, which carry
       intervals measured from the device's own deltas and so are unmoved --
       and the glitch report that used to ride along with the keying.
       
       start_sidetone and stop_sidetone had no other callers and are gone.
       The report now goes through Audio.report_glitches, which unlike
       start_sidetone does not dereference a singleton that may not be there.
       
       Simulated over both host buffer shapes, three thread hops and eight
       block grid phases: the heard delay is flat at the audio's own floor,
       24.00ms against 24.29/25.71/29.21ms before, with every KZD and KZU
       byte-identical and the rendered intervals unchanged.
   
    vfo: Simplify the VFO code
       No change in behavior.
       
       The RIT is the same widget as the VFO with something else inside it:
       both put a fixed-width number in a DigitEntry whose digit columns are
       the tuning steps, underline the step in use, and take clicks, arrow and
       page keys and the wheel the same way. That half is now SteppedEntry,
       which both derive from, and what differs is named rather than written
       twice: value() and set_value() for what is being counted, set_bg() for
       how each colours itself, and steps and step_indexes for the tables the
       clamps are taken from.
       
       VFO keeps a suffix as RIT already did, both limits are clamped with one
       max/min, the colour methods choose a colour and set it once, and
       DigitEntry's key handling goes through a small helper rather than
       repeating itself five times.
   
    audio: Simplify the device code
       No change in behavior.
       
       The accessors for the device in use -- its info, name, index, the
       names of all of them, its sample rate, and setting it by name -- were
       written once for the input and again for the output. Each is now one
       method taking the direction, with the input_ and output_ names kept as
       wrappers over it. device_info_by_name, reopen_stream and
       sd_format_supported likewise replace a pair each.
       
       Enumeration asked each device what it could do in one direction and
       then the other in two nearly identical blocks; enumerate_device is
       called once per direction instead, and returns the last rate tried so
       that the output block is sized from the output pass as before.
   
    settings: Simplify the settings code
       No change in behavior, but for platform_settings_dirname now raising
       Exception rather than the undefined name exception on a platform that
       is none of Linux, macOS and Windows.
       
       getint and getfloat were the same method twice and now share
       get_number, get_section returns the section as a dict rather than
       building one, and load's obsolete options and the audio device names
       carried over to the main settings are each handled in a loop.
       
       In the settings widgets, _titled_grid and _settings_entry replace the
       group box and field written out for the name, address, password and
       proxy, remote_settings builds its rows in a loop, and the input and
       output audio device settings -- one method each before -- share
       audio_device_settings, asking Audio about whichever direction they are
       for, as the exclusive mode setting already did. _labeled_combobox
       replaces the combobox call repeated with its colours five times.
   
    macros: Simplify the Macros tab code
       No change in behavior.
       
       Macros and CW are both TextButtons windows, and Macros repeated CW's
       code almost line for line: the dialog and its notebook, the whole
       Configure tab, the geometry saved and restored, and the settings and
       button lines read and written. That code moves into TextButtons, as
       open_dialog, add_config_tab, set_geometry, save_geometry,
       load_button_grid_settings, load_button_settings, save_settings_section
       and split_button_value, all keyed to a settings_section attribute, and
       both tabs use it. _plain_frame, _config_button and the entry-to-integer
       reading move up there too.
       
       Macros keeps what is its own: the !MACROS enable, its buttons tab, the
       F-key shortcuts, sending a macro, and the geometry recheck on first
       show.
   
    cw: Simplify the CW tab code
       No change in behavior.
       
       The dit and dah halves of the paddle note, paddle signal and memory
       delay settings are built and handled by one set of methods rather than
       two, as are auto character and auto word spacing, and the main and sub
       decode dropdowns' selections are generated from their TD/TD$ base
       rather than written out. The main and sub APF toggles and the spot
       buttons are built in a loop.
       
       _plain_frame, _dropdown, _dropdown_frame, _labeled_combobox,
       _add_cw_text, _config_button, _keyer_setting and _port_selections
       replace code repeated across the tab, the responses subscribed to are
       one table used for both subscribing and unsubscribing, and the decode
       enable, save and cancel handlers and set_geometry's clamp are shorter.
   
    panadapter: Simplify the panadapter code
       No change in behavior, but for the order a newly opened window is sent
       its cached #SCL and #FRZ/#FXA/#FXT values in, which set independent
       controls.
       
       The window's comboboxes, buttons, plots, bandwidth shading, marker
       lines and timers are each built by one helper rather than written out
       at every use. The span and scale comboboxes share _select_preset, the
       VFO A and B markers share _move_vfo_lines, and _PanControlFilter tests
       whether an event is for the window once rather than three times.
       
       monitor_loop and PanadapterChannel.on_message dispatch through tables
       rather than if/elif chains, open_window subscribes to
       relayed_cat_bases in a loop, and the frame and IPC length fields are
       read with int.from_bytes.
   
    audio: Simplify the sidetone code
       No change in behavior.
       
       The sidetone state __init__ sets and reset_sidetone clears again is
       set in one place, clear_sidetone_state. key_up_due and next_key_edge
       share peek_key_event for the one-edge lookahead and key_edge_offset
       for where an edge lands in a block, and block_dac_time holds the
       choice of DAC time and why. mix_sidetone and fade_in_k4 share
       envelope_ramp and raised_cosine rather than each writing the ramp and
       the raised cosine out, and mix_sidetone folds the pending-edge test
       into idle rather than repeating it.
   
    keyer: Simplify the keying code
       No change in behavior.
       
       send_iambic and send_ultimatic ran the same start, confirm, wait,
       decide and end loop; it is now send_burst, and each keeps only its own
       choice of element. start_element's catch-up bookkeeping moves to
       plan_sidetone_catchup and its auto spacing to wait_for_auto_space, and
       its first-element and later-element paths are one if/else rather than
       three tests of first_element. The inherent lag measurement moves out
       of end_element into measure_inherent_sidetone_lag.
       
       sleep_until, whole_ms and straight_key_ms replace code repeated across
       the keyer, the iambic memories fold into dit and dah directly, and two
       unused variables in the auto spacing go. key_sidetone reads the clock
       through stream_time, and placed_sidetone_down replaces a lookup
       repeated in three places.
       
       Also drop a debug print in Keyer.__init__ that raised TypeError
       whenever no inherent_sidetone_lag had been saved.
   
    Raise maximum volume of K4 and sidetone audio

    Version 2.0-rc14

    keyer: Take back an element whose paddles came off during the gap
       Whether there is to be another element used to be decided as the one
       before it ended, which left the operator none of the gap -- nor any of
       the lag that element is still being heard with -- to come off the
       paddles in.
       
       An element after a burst's first is now queued as before, and then
       asked about again as late as its sidetone can still be taken back:
       shortly before the output callback fills the block its key down lands
       in, around the element's own grid start. If neither the paddles nor
       the iambic memory want it by then, Audio.cancel_sidetone_down has the
       callback drop the key down, no KZD is sent, and the burst ends. A key
       down the callback had already placed is heard anyway, and the element
       goes ahead in full.
       
       sidetone_up_margin_seconds becomes sidetone_edge_margin_seconds, as it
       now covers taking an edge back as well as queueing one.
   
    Version 2.0-rc13

    set: output_buffer_time=.004, output_extra_latency=.002

    straight key: Ask for sidetone edges a block and a lead ahead
       A straight key's edges are reported as they happen. Asked for now, an
       edge was too late for its frame whenever the callback had filled that
       block just before, and sidetone_lag grew by the shortfall, rendering
       the interval before it that much long. The lag climbed an edge at a
       time over the first elements of every burst, stretching them by up to
       a block, or more where the host fills several blocks at once.
       
       key_sidetone now asks for each edge the furthest lead a block has
       lately been filled with, and a block more, ahead of now: past where the
       block carrying it could already have been filled. The lag is taken at
       the first edge, and every interval after is rendered as keyed.
   
    Take a burst's starting lag from its first key down
       A burst's lag is set when the output callback places its first key
       down, after start_element has read the delay for that element. That
       reading -- whatever the burst before left, or nothing at all before the
       first edge since startup -- was kept as the delay seen, and at the next
       element the difference between it and the real lag was taken whole as a
       change in the delay and cut out of the first gap: the whole lag, some
       20 to 30 ms, in the first burst after startup, and a few ms in later
       ones.
       
       The element after a burst's first now takes the delay seen from how far
       that first key down was actually displaced. sidetone_delay() follows
       the last edge placed of either kind rather than the last key up, so it
       no longer returns the previous burst's figure until the new burst's
       first key up has been placed.
   
    ultimatic: Decide each element when its sidetone is heard
       As send_iambic does: start the element, wait until its key down
       reaches the DAC, and read the paddles then to decide whether it is a
       dit or a dah. What they asked for as it started stands if they have
       come off by then.
   
    iambic: Decide each element when its sidetone is heard
       send_iambic starts an element, waits until its key down reaches the
       DAC, and only then reads the paddles to decide whether it is a dit or a
       dah. The operator works the paddles to what the sidetone says, so that
       is the reading that belongs to the element. The earlier reading still
       decides whether there is an element at all, and stands if the paddles
       have come off by the time it is heard.
       
       The output callback records where it placed each key down, and
       Audio.sidetone_down_heard_at turns that into a perf_counter time.
       
       The wait is capped so that a dit's key up is still queued before the
       block carrying it is filled. Audio.note_block_timing keeps the furthest
       lead any block has lately been filled with, which covers hosts that
       fill several blocks at once for a longer period of their own, as
       WASAPI does in shared mode.
   
    Split send_element into start_element and end_element
       start_element schedules the sidetone's key down and sends KZD without
       knowing whether the element is a dit or a dah; end_element is told,
       schedules the key up and sends KZU. Audio.schedule_sidetone becomes
       schedule_sidetone_down and schedule_sidetone_up, the up edge put on the
       clock offset the down edge read.
       
       The element sleep runs from when the KZD went out, so time taken
       between the two calls comes out of the element rather than stretching
       it.
   
    Fix a few debug prints

    Renable mute

    Take the receive audio's fade in to 8ms
       Four was enough to take the step out; eight makes the return gentler
       still, the worst jump out of silence going from 0.0028 to 0.0007 over
       the same sweep of sixteen phases.
       
       It has to finish in the gap between elements, which is the inter-element
       interval less the K4's own key down delay, and eight does so with room
       to spare at any speed the keyer sends at: 28ms of gap at 30 WPM with a
       12ms delay, 18 at 40 WPM with the same, both fully up well before the
       next element.  So the band is still heard at full strength between
       elements.  The comment says so, rather than leaving the figure looking
       picked out of the air.
   
    Bring the receive audio up after a silence rather than stepping in
       Reported: with the K4 receiving a loud signal, an objectionable click
       where the audio comes back after a sidetone.
       
       The mute is still in use -- the keyer takes it for the K4's own key down
       delay every element -- and k4_mute_count goes on dropping packets past
       the unmute, so the audio resumes several blocks later, from silence, at
       whatever instantaneous value the signal is at.  A loud one makes a step
       of most of full scale out of that, which is the click.  The sidetone's
       fall does not cover it: by then the fall is over.
       
       fade_in_k4 ramps the block as it is written, before the sidetone is
       mixed over it, and silence is what starts the ramp over -- a block the
       queue did not fill at all, or only partly.  So it covers a mute ending,
       a dropped packet and a queue that ran dry alike, rather than the mute
       alone.  Four milliseconds, the same raised cosine the sidetone's
       envelope uses, with the step recomputed from the device's rate where
       frames_per_output_buffer already is.  Fully up, it returns on a float
       comparison, which is where the audio is for all but a few milliseconds
       of its life.
       
       Measured through the real callback at 0.8 amplitude, muting for 10ms and
       sweeping the mute across sixteen phases of the received waveform: the
       worst jump out of silence goes from 0.8000 to 0.0028 and the worst first
       sample from 0.8000 to 0.0001.  What is left is the waveform's own slope.
       
       Not the old K4 packet fade, which shaped every packet either side of a
       mute and went with the mute rework.  Nothing is shaped here but the
       first few milliseconds after a silence.
   
    Render the sidetone once and slice it, rather than a sine per block
       numpy.sin over every frame was the largest thing in the output
       callback's keyed path, and the tone it computes is the same few
       milliseconds over and over.
       
       Render it in generate_sidetone_envelopes, where the rest of the shape is
       built and where a pitch or volume change already rebuilds it off the
       callback's thread.  What is rendered is one whole repeat, not one cycle:
       a cycle is a whole number of samples only where the pitch divides the
       rate, so it takes sample_rate // gcd of them -- 80 for 600Hz at 48k, 480
       for 700Hz, 4800 at the worst a 10Hz pitch step can ask for.  Rendered
       that way it is exact, where a table indexed by a rounded phase would
       carry its own quantizing noise.  The volume goes in it, and it travels
       in the same tuple the callback takes atomically.
       
       Tiled 8192 frames past the end so a block is one contiguous slice
       wherever in the repeat it starts, numpy's wrapping indexers costing more
       than the sine did.  sidetone_cycle_block covers a block longer than that
       margin, which nothing has asked for, rather than reading short.
       
       Same 200ms element rendered both ways differs by 3.90e-02 sample for
       sample and 4.05e-03 after shifting one sample -- the old code put a
       block's first sample at the phase plus one step, this starts at the step
       -- the remainder being the envelope, which is not shifted with it.  Tone
       peak, amplitude and largest sample step agree to five figures.
       mix_sidetone on a keyed block goes from 26.50us to 19.87us, and the tone
       generation alone from 3.79us to 0.21us.
       
       What is left is mostly the envelope's own cosine over every frame.  It
       could go the same way, but unlike the tone it would not be exact.
   
    Let the receive audio play on under a rising sidetone
       Reported: with the K4's receive audio loud, an objectionable click at
       the start of every element.
       
       The rise did start from the last sample that went out -- that is what
       sidetone_hold_sample was for -- but it then held that one value as flat
       DC for the whole four milliseconds of the rise and let the crossfade
       take it down.  Quiet audio holds a value near zero and nothing is heard.
       Loud audio caught near a peak holds most of full scale, and half an
       amplitude of DC lasting four milliseconds is the click.
       
       Draw from the queue while the envelope is still coming up, then, and let
       the crossfade bring the tone up over what is really there.  Nothing is
       drawn once the tone is fully up, the crossfade having the audio at
       nothing by then, so an element costs the block or two under its own rise
       and no more -- audio that would have been heard had the tone not covered
       it.  sidetone_hold_sample goes, and with it previous_k4_sample and
       mix_sidetone's previous_sample argument, which existed to seed the hold
       where an edge landed on frame 0.
       
       Measured through the real callback against the previous commit, receive
       audio at 0.8, sweeping the key down across sixteen phases of it and
       taking the worst DC offset over the rise: 0.4267 holding the sample,
       0.0762 letting the audio run.  What is left is the sine's own average
       over a window shorter than its period.  The worst case falls where it
       should, with the key landing near a peak.
       
       The fall is unchanged and still crossfades back into live audio from the
       frame the key comes up.
   
    An S-unit scale on the panadapter trace, and a way off the edge
       S-units on the left of the trace, where the eye goes first, and the dB
       the same signal reads in on the right.  S9 is -73 dBm on HF by the
       IARU's reckoning, every S-unit below it 6 dB down, and above it the
       marks are in dB over S9; the frames the K4 sends are already in dBm, so
       the two scales are the same numbers read two ways.  A radio reporting
       something else, or VHF's -93, wants s9_dbm moved and nothing else.
       
       The marks are thinned to suit the scale.  Six dB apart reads well over
       the forty the display usually spans and smears into a band over the
       hundred and sixty it opens at, so past ten marks they go every second or
       third, counted down from S9 so that S9 is always among them.  Worked out
       again from sigYRangeChanged, which _update_vscale trips every five
       seconds, and once at the end of __init__ -- the Y range is set before
       that connection exists to hear about it.
       
       An axis at either edge squares the display up as well.  Each insets the
       trace's plot area by its own width, and pyqtgraph keeps the waterfall's
       X view aligned with the trace's pixel for pixel, so the waterfall now
       shows bare ground in a strip at either side rather than in one hanging
       off the left.
       
       The frequency labels move with that.  Left and right sit inside the
       width their own axis reserved, at twice the margin they had -- the top
       row carries both a mark and a frequency, and two pixels between them
       read as touching -- and the center label is centred on the plot area
       rather than on the widget, which was half the difference between the two
       axes out while only one of them was there to notice.
       
       Those strips take a click, too.  There is no pixel to have been aiming
       at in them, so a click means half a display along -- down on the left,
       up on the right -- which in Track mode brings the band just beyond the
       edge clicked into the middle.  Nothing is sent before a VFO frequency is
       known to step from.
   
    Version 2.0-rc12

    iambic: Adjust paddle events by inherent_sidetone_lag

    Carry the lag floor across sessions
       inherent_sidetone_lag started at zero every session and was replaced
       outright by each measurement.  Neither is right: the figure is worth
       carrying from one session to the next, and one measurement is worth less
       than the figure already held.
       
       A trace of one session settles which figure to carry.  sidetone_delay is
       flat to a few microseconds within a burst and steps between them,
       climbing 13, 14, 15, 16, 17ms while the sending goes on and dropping
       back to 12 or 13 after each idle, where mix_sidetone's reset lets the
       next burst's first edge ask the device afresh.  Two quantities in one
       column: the floor the device wants with nothing to catch up, and the
       ratchet the catch-up exists to absorb.  Averaged together they describe
       nothing -- the mixture stood at 17.05ms at one point in that session and
       13.09 at the end, so what it reported depended on when the app was
       closed.  The floor is the half that describes the device, and the half
       inherent_sidetone_lag is for.
       
       So the floor is what is written, as [Keyer] inherent_sidetone_lag in the
       main settings -- the device and the machine it is on being the same
       whichever radio is worked -- and what the keyer is seeded with at
       startup.  Each measurement moves the held figure a quarter of the way
       rather than replacing it, a single look at a device that varies being
       worth about that much against one already held.  The first measurement
       is taken whole where nothing was restored, there being nothing to weigh
       it against, and nothing is written where nothing was measured or
       restored: an absent setting says that honestly where a zero would not.
       
       Replayed with five floor measurements: seeded at 12.8 it holds 12.37,
       12.52, 12.61, 12.48, 12.52 and writes 12.52; cold it takes the first
       whole and converges over the session.  The trace lines that produced the
       numbers above are left in.
   
    Round up element timings instead of a true round

    Version 2.0-rc11

    Adjust rx audio queue size and output_buffer_time

    Record the floor under extra_output_latency
       Two milliseconds of depth underruns on the device these figures were
       settled against, so the four is a measured floor rather than a round
       number, and the comment now says so.  The block and the depth hold each
       other up: neither reads as a sensible figure alone.
   
    Cover a late callback with depth rather than a bigger block
       With QSK the radio drops back to receive in every inter-element gap, and
       the opus decode and the CAT responses that come with the switch arrive
       together and hold the GIL the output callback needs.  Measured: eleven
       to thirteen underflows over forty seconds of sending, one at a time,
       seven of them 11 to 37ms after a key up -- inside the gaps at 30 WPM,
       not after the sending.  Semi break-in produced none at all, and closing
       the panadapter changed nothing, so it is the receive restart itself.
       
       No block size answers that, a stall being longer than a block either
       way.  What answers it is what is already queued ahead of the DAC while
       the callback is away, so ask PortAudio for the device's own low latency
       figure and extra_output_latency on top of it, in place of latency='low'.
       
       Dale settled the two figures together: a 2ms block with 4ms of added
       depth reports no underflows where 4ms and 8ms blocks both underran about
       once every three seconds.
       
       The depth is not free and the cost is uniform.  A burst's first key down
       cannot be scheduled ahead, so it is heard a whole stream latency late,
       and that is the lag inherent_sidetone_lag settles at and the keyer then
       holds -- every element is heard later by what was added, not just the
       first.
       
       Checked here: a device reporting 8.71ms of low output latency negotiates
       8.00ms against 'low' and 16.00ms against that figure plus ten, and a
       device index that will not answer falls back to 'low' rather than
       raising.
   
    Say where each output underflow fell
       The counters are totals and the report is rate limited to a line a
       second, so a burst of underflows during a burst of sending could as
       easily surface after it.  There was no telling from the output whether
       they came with the key down or after it came up.
       
       The callback notes each one -- the block's first frame, the frames since
       the key last came up, and whether the key was down at the time -- and
       whoever reports next prints them.  Nothing is printed from the callback
       itself, for the reason already in the comment there: a status flag means
       that thread is late, and stderr is the last place to spend what is left.
       The subtraction is done at the callback because last_key_up_frame moves
       on and the report may be a second behind.  last_key_up_frame is set
       where mix_sidetone applies an up edge, in the same frame counter the
       callback keeps, so no clock is read on the audio thread.  The deque is
       bounded, nothing guaranteeing anyone comes to read it.
       
       Through the real callback and mix_sidetone with a 20ms element and
       underflows planted at three blocks: the one inside the tone reads with
       the key down, the two after read 12.0 and 20.0ms, being 576 and 960
       frames past an up edge at frame 960, and one before anything was keyed
       says so.
       
       It has already paid for itself.  With the 4ms block, six of thirteen
       underflows were with the key down; at 8ms only two of eleven, the tone's
       own sin and envelope over a block being what did not fit.  What is left
       clusters 11 to 37ms after a key up, which at 30 WPM is inside the
       inter-element gap rather than after the sending -- the QSK receive
       window, where the decode, the CAT responses and the panadapter all start
       again at once.
   
    Disable mute and see how this is received
       When QSK is on, there is an artifact: an echo after unkeying for
       a couple of seconds.
   
    Draw receive audio for the block the key comes up in
       The block the key up landed in was still keyed when the drawing was
       decided, so it took nothing and the first milliseconds of the fall mixed
       against silence, the receive audio returning a block late.
       
       key_up_due answers the question before the queue is drawn on.  The
       arithmetic is next_key_edge's, against the same clock: an offset below
       the block's frame count is that method's own test for landing here, an
       edge coming out negative being bumped to the earliest frame the block
       has left.  To look at the edge it takes it off the queue and leaves it
       in pending_key_event, where next_key_edge looks first -- the one place
       lookahead it already keeps for an edge belonging to a later block, so
       nothing is consumed that was not going to be.
       
       The same 20ms element through the real mix_sidetone: the key up block
       now draws its chunk and ends at 0.448 where it ended at 0.073, the fall
       mixing into receive audio from the frame the key came up rather than a
       block later, and the block after carries it to 0.500.  The tone's own
       blocks still leave the queue at five chunks.
       
       The condition short circuits on sidetone_keyed, so key_up_due is only
       ever reached with the key down and cannot pull an edge into
       pending_key_event during an idle stretch, where mix_sidetone reads that
       same field to decide whether to reset sidetone_lag.
   
    Leave the receive audio queue alone while the key is down
       Every block drew its samples from k4_decoded_rx_audio_queue whether or
       not anything would be heard of them.  While the key is down nothing is:
       mix_sidetone writes sidetone_hold_sample over whatever the callback put
       there, and the crossfade at the end covers what is left.  So the audio
       the K4 sent was taken out of the queue and dropped on the floor, a
       block at a time, for as long as the operator was sending.
       
       Take nothing while sidetone_keyed, and let what is in the queue be
       played after.  output_stream_leftover stays put too, samples already
       drawn but not yet played being no different.
       
       Only while the key is down.  Once it is up the tone falls away over
       several milliseconds and the crossfade brings the K4 audio back in
       underneath it, so that audio has to be there to come back to, and the
       frames after the fall are the K4's alone.
       
       Rendered through the real mix_sidetone over a queue carrying a steady
       0.5: the tone blocks leave the queue at five chunks throughout, the
       block the fall finishes in comes in at 0.441 and reaches 0.500 by its
       end, and every block after it is 0.500.
       
       What remains: the block the key up lands in was still keyed when the
       drawing was decided, so the first milliseconds of the fall mix against
       silence and the receive audio returns a block late -- under 4ms, once an
       element, just after the radio stopped transmitting.  Closing it means
       working out before the drawing whether an up edge falls inside the
       block, which is the placement arithmetic next_key_edge already does.
   
    Don't start audio out until K4's rx queue is full

    Set K4's rx audio queue to 100ms to avoid dropouts

    Rename K4 audio queues, threads and loops

    Quiet stderr by pointing fd 2 away, not by closing it
       Reported: with a panadapter open at startup, sometimes Popen fails with
       EBADF from inside _close_pipe_fds.
       
       Closing fd 2 does not just silence stderr, it frees the number.  The
       four PortAudio wrappers closed it while asking what a device supports,
       and anything another thread opened in that window was handed the lowest
       free descriptor, which was 2.  restore_open_windows spawns the
       panadapter from the Qt thread while enumeration probes formats on
       another: Popen's pipe gets fd 2, the probe's finally dup2s stderr back
       over it, and Popen finds its own descriptor gone.  Only at startup do
       the two overlap, which is the sometimes.
       
       quiet_stderr points fd 2 at /dev/null for the duration instead, so the
       number is never on offer.  All four wrappers are three lines each now
       and no os.close(2) is left.  The comment on the private stderr said it
       was for printing while fd 2 is closed; it is for printing while fd 2 is
       pointed elsewhere.
       
       Shown, each case in its own process: under the old wrapper a pipe opened
       during the window is fd 2 outright, no threads needed, where the new one
       hands out 12 and 13; and probing while spawning for five seconds raises
       OSError(9, 'Bad file descriptor') the old way and nothing the new way.
       A write to fd 2 inside the block still goes nowhere and one after it
       still comes through.
   
    React more quickly to the need for sidetone catchup

    Round sidetone length to nearest millisecond

    Version 2.0-rc10

    Make a capture stream prove itself as a playback one does
       Reported from Windows, on all three exclusive attempts: active=False,
       stopped=True, immediately after start returned success.  PortAudio
       brought the stream's thread up and lost it before the first block, three
       different buffers deep -- 7.00, 13.00 and 21.33ms of negotiated latency
       -- with polling failing the same way as event driven.  That is below
       anything this end can pass an argument about, but it is a signal worth
       having.
       
       stream_is_dead is that signal: a stream just started reporting itself
       stopped, which is the one thing it cannot honestly be.  Only ever taken
       as proof of death -- audio moving is what proves life, so a device slow
       to its first block is untouched -- and it turns a quarter second of
       waiting into nothing at all, three times over.
       
       The input side gets the ladder and a check of its own.  Nothing calls us
       there, so what says a capture stream is running is frames arriving to be
       read, and death is asked about first: a stopped stream still holds what
       it took in before it stopped, and read_available alone would read that
       as running.  It matters more here than on the output.  A dead exclusive
       capture stream does not cost delay, it puts the operator off the air:
       the no-input warning fires and the input thread returns, where falling
       back to shared would have kept him transmitting.
       
       open_attempts is now one builder for both directions, and carries with
       it the two diagnostics that produced the report above: which attempt
       failed to open and what PortAudio said about it, instead of an exception
       swallowed in silence, and what a stream that opened and did nothing
       thought of itself.
       
       Checked against real streams: a live capture stream reads as capturing
       in 10ms, a stopped one as dead in 0.0, a closed one likewise; a stopped
       playback stream now fails in 0.0 where it waited 254, and a live but
       silent one still waits the clock out, as it must.  Both ladders build
       alike -- flags 1 at 192 frames, flags 1 at the device period, flags 9 at
       the device period, then shared.
   
    Try exclusive mode three ways before giving it up
       Reported from Windows: with the rate probed exclusively and 192 frames
       asked for, the open still succeeds and sounddevice is never asked for
       audio.  That says the rate and the format are acceptable to the driver
       and what is missing is the callback.  PortAudio's exclusive path waits
       on an event the driver is to set every period, and drivers exist that
       take the open, start, and never set it.
       
       paWinWasapiPolling is the cure for that one: PortAudio drives the device
       from its own thread and asks nothing of the driver but the buffer.
       sounddevice has no argument for the flag, so it goes on the struct it
       builds, guarded -- a build that has moved the struct is a build without
       this attempt in it.
       
       Rather than test one guess at a time, walk a ladder and keep whatever
       plays: ours at 192 frames, the device's own period, the same again with
       the driver's event taken out, then shared.  Every exclusive attempt buys
       the same delay and they differ only in what they ask of the driver.  Each
       still has to prove it plays before it is kept, so one that lies its way
       through an open is found out and the next is tried; shared is last and
       is asked to prove nothing, being what there is.  The debug line names
       which won, at what block and latency.
       
       Checked: the attempts build as flags 1 at 192, flags 1 at the device
       period, flags 9 at the device period, then shared, with each attempt
       holding its own settings object so polling does not leak into the first;
       and blocksize 0 opens here for real and hands the callback varying frame
       counts, which it takes as it is given.
       
       Costs: a device that fails all three delays the open by about a second,
       the liveness check being a quarter second each, and only where the
       setting is on.  With blocksize 0 frames_per_output_buffer no longer
       describes the real block, so the warm-up may render a different size --
       harmless, the ramp grows once.
   
    Ask for the rate and the block a stream is opened with
       Two things a stream needs settled before it opens, both of which differ
       between the modes it may open in.
       
       The rate the device was enumerated at was tested in shared mode, where
       the Windows mixer resamples whatever it is handed; exclusive mode has
       none of that and the hardware itself has to run at what is asked for.
       So test again with the settings the open will carry, once per attempt --
       exclusive asks the driver, the shared attempt behind it asks the mixer
       and so comes back to what enumeration found.  With the minimum and the
       maximum both 48000 there is one rate to try, so today this settles the
       block and says, with debug on, where the device will not give that rate
       on those terms; it has a choice to make again if that range widens.
       
       The block goes with it.  In exclusive mode the device period is the
       driver's to fix and the buffer has to be a whole number of them, so a
       duration is the wrong thing to ask with: exclusive_blocksize is a frame
       count, 192, which is the 4ms wanted at 48000.  The output stream already
       opens with frames_per_output_buffer, and warm_sidetone renders after the
       choice, so both follow the attempt.  The input stream asks for the same
       count where it opens exclusively.
       
       frames_per_output_buffer was derived from output_buffer_time and
       output_buffer_time then re-derived from it.  That round trip is exact
       while the block is a duration, but a forced frame count walks it -- 192
       frames at 24000 would redefine the block as 8ms and the next shared
       derivation would ask for 192 again.  output_buffer_time is left as the
       duration wanted, and output_blocksize is the one place that answers how
       many frames to ask for.
       
       Checked against the module: 192 frames and 4.00ms shared at 48000, 96
       and 4.00ms shared at 24000, 192 for exclusive at either; alternating the
       two modes three times leaves the rate, the frame count and
       output_buffer_time where they started; and a device refusing 48000
       exclusively changes nothing and says so.
   
    Always request 48000 Hz for audio devices

    Default exclusive mode to "No"

    Make an exclusive output stream prove it is playing
       Reported from Windows: with exclusive mode available and enabled, the
       speakers produce nothing.  I cannot reproduce it here, having no WASAPI
       to open, so rather than guess at the cause make the failure show itself.
       A WASAPI device can take an exclusive open, start without complaint, and
       then never call its callback at all, which is exactly what opening fine
       and playing nothing looks like.
       
       output_stream_playing gives a newly started stream a quarter of a second
       to ask for its first block.  The callback runs whether or not the K4 is
       sending -- an empty queue is filled with silence -- so one block asked
       for is the whole of the evidence.  Only an attempt with something left
       to fall back to is asked for it; the shared open is what there is, dead
       or alive.  The close takes output_stream_lock, as every other close in
       that loop does, the keyer reading the stream's clock.
       
       A live stream costs 10ms at the open, a dead one the full quarter
       second.  Checked against the module with a fake callback: a frame
       counter that moves reads True in 10ms, one that never moves False after
       253ms, one that takes 120ms to start True at 122.
       
       So the next run says which fault this is.  Sound back, with the warning
       that exclusive mode was not available, means the device was taking the
       open and playing nothing, and it now falls back on its own.  Still
       silent and no warning means the callback is running and the audio is
       going somewhere the speakers are not, which is a different fault.
       
       Left alone: the input side, where a dead exclusive stream shows up as a
       blocking read or the existing no-input warning rather than as silence,
       and the 4ms block we ask for, which need not be what the device's
       exclusive period wants.
   
    Say so when exclusive mode was asked for and not had
       Shared mode works, just further behind, so nothing about the audio tells
       the operator he did not get what he asked for.  Warn him, once for each
       device direction, where the stream opened shared with exclusive mode
       set.  Both remedies are his: close whatever else is holding the device,
       or turn the setting off and stop expecting the lower delay.
       
       Only where it was asked for, so never with the setting off and never off
       Windows, and only where the stream did open -- a device that would not
       open at all already has its own warning.
       
       An exclusive open clears the flag again.  What he was told was about one
       device refusing at one moment, so the next device to refuse, or the same
       one once whatever held it has let go, is his to hear about too.
       
       Checked with six opens on the output side and a device granting
       exclusive in the middle: warned, silent, silent, cleared, warned again,
       silent.  The input flag stays put through all of it.  The call sites
       themselves are unreachable here, this machine having no WASAPI for the
       settings object to come from.
       
       Still silent: turning the setting off and on again keeps the flag, so
       the same device refusing after a toggle says nothing more.
   
    Offer exclusive mode for the input device too
       The microphone crosses the same Windows mixer on the way in, and what it
       costs there comes off what the far end hears.  input_loop opens the way
       output_loop does now, exclusive first and shared after it, compares an
       input_stream_generation alongside the device index, and says which mode
       it got and at what latency.  It comes back around on every frame it
       reads, so a change takes hold at once.
       
       wasapi_exclusive_settings takes the direction and reads its own setting,
       which renames the output's key from wasapi_exclusive to
       wasapi_exclusive_output.  Both still default to yes, so a stored setting
       that was never turned off means the same thing either way.
       
       The tab puts each device above its own setting -- input, exclusive
       input, output, exclusive output -- with rows counted rather than
       numbered, so anywhere but Windows the two are absent and the layout
       stands as it was.
       
       Also quieted the input retry loop, which printed a traceback for every
       failed open: a refused exclusive attempt is ordinary and is what the
       fallback is for, so only the shared attempt, whose failure means the
       device is not to be had, reports now.  The comment there pointed at a
       matching comment in output_loop that no longer exists.
       
       Checked against the module with wasapi_available forced true: each
       setting gates its own direction and defaults to exclusive unset, each
       combobox writes only its own key and reopens only its own stream, and
       the generations move independently.  With a real InputStream, a refused
       exclusive open raises PortAudioError and the shared open after it
       succeeds with nothing printed.
   
    Let the operator turn exclusive output mode off
       Holding the device exclusively is not always what is wanted -- nothing
       else on the machine can play while the stream is open -- so put it on
       the Audio tab, under the output device it applies to, in the Yes/No
       style the tab already uses.  It appears only where WASAPI does, there
       being no other host API with an exclusive mode to ask about, and
       defaults to yes, which is what the previous commit shipped.
       
       The mode is settled where the device is opened, so the stream has to go
       down and come up again for a change to mean anything.  output_loop owns
       the stream -- closing one under it from another thread is what
       output_stream_lock is there to prevent -- so reopen_output_stream only
       marks it stale by bumping a generation the loop compares alongside the
       device index, and the loop obliges on the next packet from the K4.
       
       Checked against the module: the gate returns the settings object for
       true, 'yes' and 'on' and None for false, 'no' and empty, the generation
       moves on a reopen and the call is silent before there is a singleton to
       mark, and the combobox builds under an offscreen Qt and calls back with
       'No' when set to it.  On this machine wasapi_available is false, so
       nothing is offered and nothing is passed.
   
    Halve the output block to 4ms
       The block is two thirds of what stands between the paddle and the
       sidetone: an edge waits for the first callback to run after it was
       keyed, so a block is that wait at worst and half a block on average, and
       the block being filled is the earliest audio still writable.
       
       On this ALSA device the negotiated stream latency tracks the blocksize
       as well -- 16.00ms at 384 frames, 8.00ms at 192 -- so the callback to
       DAC time halves with it.  Through next_key_edge, with a key down 2ms
       before the callback, the lag comes out 10.00ms where it was 18.00 at the
       old block and 26.00 before the margin went, and the worst case, an edge
       keyed a whole block early, 12.00ms against 24.00.
       
       Nothing else assumed the old block: the callback keeps
       output_stream_leftover, so a 4ms block takes 4ms of a 20ms K4 packet and
       carries the rest forward, and k4_mute_count counts packets.
       
       This is the one figure here that can cost more than it saves.  The
       callback has 4ms to run now rather than 8, and one underrun puts more
       delay into the sidetone than the block ever took out.  Four seconds on
       an idle machine gave 1006 callbacks and no underruns, which proves
       little; report_glitches counts them with debug on, and .008 is the way
       back.
   
    Open the output device exclusively where WASAPI allows it
       Shared mode mixes this stream with everything else the machine is
       playing, and the Windows mixer's own engine period -- ten milliseconds
       or so -- is the floor on how soon a block can reach the DAC, whatever
       blocksize is asked for.  Exclusive mode hands the device to this process
       alone and takes that to a few milliseconds, and every one of them comes
       off the delay between the paddle and the sidetone, which is what the
       keyer then has to hold or work off.
       
       Exclusive first, shared after it.  A device need not take the format
       exclusively and another application may be holding it that way already,
       and each mode keeps the retry loop it had, so a device busy for a moment
       after a close still gets its tries.  A debug line says which mode was
       had and what latency PortAudio negotiated for it.
       
       Verified on Linux, which exercises everything but the exclusive open
       itself: with no WASAPI host API the settings come out None, the attempt
       list is the single shared open it always was, and extra_settings=None
       opens as before.  Forcing the settings onto an ALSA stream is refused
       with PaErrorCode -9984, an ordinary exception, so a Windows device that
       will not take the format falls through to shared rather than leaving no
       audio at all.  The exclusive path is unmeasured until it runs there.
       
       Note that no other application can play to the device while this holds
       it exclusively.
   
    Drop the block of margin from the sidetone lag
       An edge too late for the frame it wanted was pushed to the start of the
       next block, a whole block past the earliest the device could still play
       it.  The margin was there because an edge reported as it happened is
       picked up by the first callback to run after it, up to a block late, and
       without cover for that the lag had to grow over a burst's first few
       elements, stretching them.
       
       Every edge but a burst's first key down is queued a whole gap ahead now
       and lands on its own frame, so the margin was covering only the one edge
       that is late by definition, and the ones a stall leaves late, which the
       keyer's re-anchor and catch-up have.  It cost a block of monitoring
       delay on every burst.  Put the edge in the earliest frame the block has
       left instead, which is what start is for.
       
       Measured against the previous commit with the real methods, 48kHz, 8ms
       blocks, a callback running 10ms ahead of the DAC and a key down 2ms
       before it: heard 12.00ms after the key rather than 20.00, and 12ms is
       that device's floor -- 10 of latency and the 2 the edge waited to be
       picked up.  An edge queued 30ms ahead is heard 12.00ms after the time
       asked for rather than 20.00, so the whole burst rides lower and not just
       its first element.  Rendering a 40ms dit through mix_sidetone block by
       block, the tone starts at 12.35ms where it started at 20.48.
   
    Version 2.0-rc9

    Print the catch-up rate only where it changes
       The line went out on every adjustment the check made, clamp included, so
       a device pinned at the cap or the floor reported the same number once a
       second for as long as it was sending.  Work the new rate out first,
       clamp it, and say nothing unless it differs from the one standing.
       
       The average that moved goes on the line with it -- the rate means little
       without what it reacted to -- and :.3g keeps 5.6 from printing as
       5.599999999999999.  Over a fourteen second ramp the whole of the output
       is now four lines, at 2, 4, 8 and 5.6.
   
    Rate the catch-up by whether it is winning, not by the lag
       The device's lag on its own cannot tell a device running away from one
       whose lag is growing while the catch-up still gains on it, and the
       second wants no more pull than it already has.  Watch what the catch-up
       has left to work off instead -- the lag over the inherent one, less what
       the schedule has already taken up -- which is the thing the pull is
       actually working against.  Range 1 to 20 now, starting at 1.
       
       In the same ramp of 5ms a second, sending characters of four dits, the
       rate goes 1, 2, 4, 8 and then holds at 8 while the device keeps
       degrading, the pull having caught up with it; the old signal doubled
       blindly to the cap there.  As the lag comes back in it eases to 5.6.
       
       Worth knowing: within one unbroken character behind can only fall --
       behind_n is behind_(n-1) less the step, a change in the audio's delay
       being absorbed whole by sidetone_delay_used and cancelling -- so a long
       continuous burst reads as winning throughout and eases to the floor
       whatever the device does.  That is right, the absorption being what
       holds it in step there.  The signal lives at character starts, where the
       offset resets and the whole lag is taken on again.
   
    Vary the catch-up rate with what the lag is doing
       A fixed rate has to be picked for the worst device it will meet.  At 20
       -- two percent off an element per millisecond behind -- it takes the lag
       out of a device that is quietly holding one just as hard as out of a
       device that is running away, and shortening elements is not free.
       
       Start at 2 instead and move between 2 and 20 by what the lag is seen to
       do.  A running average of the lag over the inherent one is looked at
       once a second: up by more than 2ms since the last look doubles the rate,
       down by more than 2ms eases it off by a third of the way, and steady
       leaves it alone.  The average is exponential over the elements, one
       sample each, since a single element's reading moves with whatever block
       boundary its edge lands near.
       
       Driven with 30 WPM dits and a device whose lag ramps 5ms a second, the
       rate goes 2, 4, 8, 16, 20 and holds at the cap; when the lag comes back
       in it eases 20, 14, 9.8, 6.86.  Ordinary recovery is gentler for it: a
       burst starting 15ms behind trims its first element by six percent and
       tapers, 37.60, 38.37, 38.89 ... 39.97, reaching the inherent lag over a
       dozen elements where before it took three at ten percent.
   
    Give up the schedule's offset at a re-anchor, don't pay for it
       Holding the re-anchored grid off by sidetone_delay_used lengthened a gap
       that is already a hole.  The edge lands at the same instant either way
       -- now, which is as early as it can be asked for -- so the offset had
       nowhere useful to go: drop it to zero and let the element's grid start
       be the edge itself.  The catch-up works it back over the elements after,
       which is what happens at the start of every burst.
       
       With the same harness, a 100ms stall that underruns the audio from 10ms
       to 25 -- a stall long enough to blow the grid has blown the callback too
       -- the gap at the stall comes out 100.0ms keyed and heard, where the
       previous commit made it 115.0.  The element after it still gets its edge
       at the instant itself rather than in the past, and the two elements
       after that carry the offset back at 36.0 and 36.5ms, the same shape the
       catch-up produces after any change in the delay.
       
       Not covered: a stall that leaves the audio alone, where the schedule was
       already running ahead of a grid the audio was keeping up with.  There
       the element after the stall cannot be heard earlier than now plus the
       delay whatever is done, so the heard gap stays long and only the keyed
       gap comes back.
   
    Keep the re-anchored grid clear of the schedule's offset
       A grid moved up to exactly now still left the sidetone asked for in the
       past: it goes in sidetone_delay_used ahead of the grid start, so the
       edge carried the offset backwards and sidetone_lag took the shortfall --
       the same ratchet the re-anchor was there to avoid, only smaller.
       
       Move the grid to now plus that offset instead, so the edge comes out at
       the current instant, and let the keying wait those few milliseconds.
       Against a gap already a whole inter-element interval long they are
       nothing.  end_element_timestamp goes with the grid, being what the sleep
       measures from, and is written afresh at the end of the element anyway.
       The offset is floored at zero: after the audio delay drops mid-burst it
       can go negative, and then there is nothing in the past to avoid.
       
       Checked with the same harness, the device at 25ms and the inherent lag
       learned at 10, so the schedule runs 15ms ahead of the grid.  Stalling an
       element's sleep by 100ms, the element after it was scheduled 15ms in the
       past on the previous commit and is scheduled at the instant itself now,
       for 15ms more of the stalled gap.  The unstalled burst comes out
       identical on both.
   
    Put the element grid back on real time after a stall
       The inter-element sleep was the only place lateness was worked off, and
       it cannot work off more than the one gap it is.  Held off longer than a
       whole inter-element interval -- the Qt thread painting a panadapter, the
       scheduler keeping an element's sleep past its length, a collection --
       the keyer arrived at the next element with its grid start already in the
       past, and time.sleep refused the negative interval outright.
       
       Clamping the sleep alone would not have done: the element sleep runs its
       full length whenever it starts, so the lateness would never be given
       back and every element after it would want a gap shorter than nothing.
       Move the grid up to now instead, and do it before the sidetone is
       scheduled -- edges asked for in the past are dragged forward by
       sidetone_lag, which shifts every edge after them, and the stall would
       become the very ratchet the catch-up exists to work off.
       
       Checked against the module with a fake clock that can return late from a
       sleep, 30 WPM dits.  An element sleep 30ms late is absorbed by the gap
       as before, all gaps still 40.0ms.  One 100ms late leaves a single 100ms
       gap where the stall was and returns to 40.0ms immediately; the same
       stall on the previous commit raises at the sleep with -60.00ms.
       
       What it does not cover: after the grid moves, the element's sidetone is
       scheduled at now less sidetone_delay_used, so a schedule that had been
       running ahead of the grid asks for that edge slightly in the past and
       sidetone_lag takes the shortfall.
   
    Measure the inherent sidetone lag after the sidetone, not before
       The lag to hold was read where the KZD went out, before the element's
       sidetone had been sent.  At that point the delay standing is the
       previous burst's -- or nothing at all, the first time through -- so the
       inherent lag was pinned at zero for the whole of a session's first
       burst, leaving the catch-up chasing a lag the device was never going to
       give up.  Remember the gap the KZD carried and take the reading at the
       end of the element instead, once the sidetone has been sent and placing
       its edges has cost the audio the lag it needs.
       
       Clamp behind at zero with it.  A schedule further forward than the lag
       standing warrants is not a debt to be paid back by stretching elements;
       without the clamp the first burst of a session, where the inherent lag
       goes from zero to its real value between elements one and two, came out
       with two elements stretched by up to ten percent.
       
       Checked against the module with a fake clock and an audio model whose
       edge placement lags the schedule.  A session's first burst now measures
       the lag off its own first element and runs 40.00ms throughout, and a
       burst that starts 15ms behind the inherent lag still trims to 36.00 and
       36.50 and settles at that lag rather than at zero.
   
    Print inherent sidetone lag value

    Hold the sidetone lag the device settles at
       The catch-up drove the lag to nothing, trimming tones and gaps at the
       opening of every character to do it.  A constant short lag is not heard
       though; only one that grows is.
       
       inherent_sidetone_lag is recorded wherever the gap sent in KZD comes out
       zero -- no previous element at all, or one measured out of range -- both
       of which mean there was no gap before this element to measure, so the
       operator has been idle and the audio has settled.  Whatever lag it has
       settled at with nothing to catch up is the least the device is going to
       manage.  behind is measured against that rather than against zero.
       
       With the audio holding at its settled lag nothing is trimmed at all and
       a character is heard a constant 10 ms late, where it used to open 36/39
       against a 40 ms ideal.  When the audio has genuinely degraded while idle
       -- settled at 10 ms, 25 ms on the first element of the next burst -- the
       catch-up runs and stops at the 10 ms held rather than at zero.
       
       Little is left for it to do: d5fa100 absorbs a delay change whole, so a
       slip mid-character costs nothing and leaves behind untouched.  What
       remains is the excess a burst opens with.  Until a baseline is recorded,
       the first burst of a session behaves as before.
   
    Print sidetone element length

    Absorb a change in the audio delay instead of walking it
       A delay that moved mid-character had its whole change walked off by the
       catch-up, so it landed in the one gap it happened across -- a 10 ms slip
       stretching a 40 ms gap to 50 -- and then trimmed the two tones after it
       working off a lag it had just taken on.
       
       The change is taken whole now, and at once.  Moving the schedule earlier
       by exactly what the audio got later leaves the timing where it was and
       costs nothing: the gap shortens by the same amount the later audio
       stretched it, and the two cancel.  A jump, a drop and a delay jittering
       every element all leave the gaps at the ideal.
       
       What the catch-up is left with is the lag a burst starts with, which is
       real -- a first element cannot be scheduled earlier than the moment it
       was keyed -- and is spread as 9cdba82 has it.
       
       sidetone_delay_used now tracks the audio delay closely rather than
       trailing it, so the sidetone is scheduled a whole delay ahead of the
       keyed time.  A delay exceeding an inter-element gap would put that
       schedule in the past, which the audio cannot honour.  That was true
       before the catch-up as well; walking it had been masking it.
   
    Spread the audio delay over several gaps, not just the first
       A burst anchored sidetone_delay_used on the delay standing, so the whole
       of it landed in the one gap between a character's first and second
       element: 30 ms against a 40 ms ideal at 30 wpm.  It starts from no
       offset at all now and the catch-up spreads it over as many elements as
       it needs -- 36 then 39 for a 10 ms delay, four gaps for 30 ms.
       
       That only works with the step taken at the element after the one that
       paid for it.  An element and the gap after it are scaled by the same
       fraction and the schedule moves earlier by what that frees up, but the
       step was sized from the current element's fraction, which had changed
       as the lag closed.  Gaps came out stretched where tones had been
       trimmed -- 42 and 41 ms.  Carrying the fraction forward one element
       leaves every tone and the gap after it scaled alike, and no gap longer
       than ideal.
       
       A character starts a whole delay late and runs momentarily fast, tones
       and gaps together, until it is on the paddle.  Elements are trimmed at
       most max_sidetone_catchup_fraction as before, and the K4 still gets the
       full lengths.
   
    Trim the first element of a character too
       The catch-up sat inside the "if not first_element" branch, so a
       character's opening tone stayed at full length while every tone after it
       was shortened to work off the lag.  That is exactly the tone that stands
       out.
       
       The block is hoisted above the branch and runs for every element.  Only
       the first element's start is still fixed -- there is no scheduling a
       burst earlier than the moment it was keyed -- and it now schedules
       sidetone_seconds rather than element_seconds.
       
       At 30 wpm, 8 ms behind: the opening tone comes out at 36 ms with the
       rest, where it used to be the one 40 ms tone among them.  With nothing
       to catch up every tone is full length, first included, as before.
       
       The gap between a character's first and second element still compresses
       by about the audio delay, the first element's start being where it is.
       Trimming it narrows that by the trim, but squaring it up properly is a
       question of how a burst anchors, not of this.
   
    Send the ideal inter-element gap in KZD
       rendered_gap_adjustment answered sidetone_lag minus the last up edge's
       displacement -- how far the lag had moved between the two edges of a
       gap.  That described what was heard when d9ef7f9 added it, a lag change
       stretching the gap by exactly that much and nothing else touching it.
       
       Two changes since made it wrong.  5c6ad55 has the catch-up absorb a lag
       change by shortening tones and gaps, so the gap no longer stretches by
       it -- it comes out short by the catch-up's own step instead.  3fa9a15
       dropped the lag cap, so the lag grows freely and the stale figure fired
       on nearly every element.  Measured against the gap actually rendered it
       disagreed in both directions, by up to 10 ms.
       
       It also cut against 5c6ad55's own policy, which keeps the air signal at
       the ideal and works the lag off in the sidetone alone.  So the ideal is
       what KZD carries now, and the adjustment goes, with the carry and the
       bounding and rounding that only ever existed to shape it -- d9ef7f9,
       2a0f491 and 72530ad together.
       
       sidetone_up_shift stays: sidetone_delay() reads it, and the catch-up
       depends on that.  The inter-character gap keeps its own 0..5000 guard,
       still measuring real elapsed time.
   
    Drop the cap on sidetone_lag
       8dd334c capped the lag and took an edge at the start of the block once
       it was reached, which stopped the ratchet but quantized placement to the
       blocksize while it did, and left the cap itself standing as monitoring
       delay.  380d794 then derived the cap from the device to make that
       residual as small as it could be.
       
       Neither is wanted now.  5c6ad55 absorbs the growth instead, walking
       sidetone_delay_used toward the lag and shortening tones and gaps to pay
       for it, so the lag is free to grow as far as it needs to and no edge is
       clamped.  next_key_edge is byte for byte what it was before 8dd334c;
       max_sidetone_lag and the figure derived at stream open go with it,
       nothing else having referred to them.
   
    Put the element grid back on real time
       9e1e451 made element_timestamp the heard start of the element and hung
       the grid off that.  It has been the wrong basis: the grid is what the
       keyer paces off, what the mute measures against and what KZD and KZU
       are derived from, and none of those have anything to do with when the
       audio comes out.  f97e422 and 0558e77 were both fallout from it.
       
       element_timestamp is real time again -- when the operator keyed the
       element -- and the audio's delay is taken off the sidetone schedule
       alone: the sidetone goes in ahead of the keyed start by the delay the
       audio will add, so it is heard when the element was keyed.  A burst's
       first element cannot be moved earlier than now, so it still carries the
       whole delay; everything after it is heard on time.
       
       end_sidetone_timestamp is computed from what the sidetone was really
       given -- its scheduled start, its length, and the delay -- rather than
       from the grid, which only equalled the heard end while the grid was on
       the heard timeline.  It stays right for an element the catch-up
       shortened and for a burst's first one.
       
       The catch-up from 5c6ad55 is unchanged.  It walks sidetone_delay_used
       as before; that figure now offsets the sidetone from a real-time grid
       instead of deriving the grid.
   
    Work the sidetone lag off by shortening tones and gaps
       The schedule took Audio.sidetone_delay() whole on every element, so an
       underrun that grew that delay moved the whole sidetone back with it and
       the next one moved it back again.  8dd334c capped how far the lag could
       ratchet; this stops the keyer chasing it in jumps at all.
       
       The delay the schedule allows for is walked toward the delay the audio
       is really adding, and each step is paid for out of the audio between
       this element and the one before: the element and the gap after it are
       both rendered short by the same fraction, and the schedule moves by
       their sum.  Taking it from the element alone would leave the gap to
       stretch by the difference.
       
       The fraction follows how far behind it is -- two percent per
       millisecond, a tenth off at 5 ms -- so it pulls hard when it is far
       behind and eases off as it arrives, and is capped at a tenth so a large
       lag is worked off over more elements rather than by cutting any one of
       them harder.  Where a whole step would overshoot the remaining lag, as
       it does at slow speeds, the last of it is split between element and gap
       in the same proportion.
       
       At 30 wpm: 5 ms behind clears in one element, 10 ms in two, 30 ms in
       four, and the shortening tapers off instead of stopping abruptly.
       
       Nothing shortened here reaches the air.  KZU still carries the full
       element_seconds and KZD the full gap, and end_element_timestamp and the
       mute still come off keyed_start, so the keyer's pacing is untouched.
   
    Version 2.0-rc8

    Never send a negative inter-character gap in KZD
       The inter-character gap had only an upper clamp, so KZD-010; went out
       whenever it measured negative.  The inter-element gap beside it has
       been floored at zero all along.
       
       It measures negative because end_element_timestamp is the previous
       element's intended keyed end -- keyed_start + element_seconds, with
       keyed_start taken off the heard grid less sidetone_delay().  The grid
       advances by a fixed amount, so a delay that shrank between two elements
       leaves keyed_start, and with it that timestamp, ahead of real time.  An
       18 ms delay dropping to 8 ms gives exactly -010.
       
       What drops it that far is the lag reset: mix_sidetone zeroes
       sidetone_lag after half a second of quiet, so a burst following a pause
       re-establishes its lag from nothing and the first up shift measured
       comes back well short of the stale one.  Hence only sometimes.
       
       The guard is now 0 <= ms <= 5000, keeping the existing convention that
       an out-of-range gap is reported as none.  Ordinary pauses are
       unaffected.
   
    Version 2.0-rc7

    Pace the keyer off the keyed end, not the heard one
       9e1e451 moved end_element_timestamp onto the heard timeline along with
       the grid, and that is the value the keyer paces off.  Both its readers
       measure it against time.perf_counter(): the inter-element sleep, and
       the inter-character gap sent in KZD.  Putting it a sidetone_delay() in
       the future made the keyer wake that much late on every element, so the
       paddle -- timestamped as it happens, on real time -- was sampled late
       against what was being sent.
       
       Only the sidetone grid belongs on the heard timeline.  element_timestamp
       and the scheduling it drives are untouched, so heard elements still come
       out exactly an element and a gap apart; the keyer samples the paddle an
       element and a gap after the element was keyed again.
       
       This also drops the extra sidetone_delay() pause after a burst's first
       element, and the inter-character gap in KZD is measured on real time
       again instead of reading a sidetone_delay() short.  end_sidetone_timestamp
       stays on the heard timeline, which is what 31df1ee wanted for the auto
       character and word space.
   
    Version 2.0-rc6

    Release the keyer device the new type no longer uses
       poll_new_device stops polling when the device type changes but never
       closes the port, so the old device stayed held: selecting a serial
       straight key or paddles and later switching to MoMIDI left the serial
       side open, and a MoMIDI adapter presenting a serial port as well could
       not then be opened.  The same the other way round.
       
       set_device_type is where the type flags settle, so it releases whichever
       of the two the new type does not use.
       
       The port goes, not the singleton.  Both are created once when the keyer
       settings are built and nothing on the enable path opens them again --
       enable_serial_device_frame and enable_midi_device_frame only show
       frames -- so dropping the singleton would strand the device for good.
       release_port closes the port, leaves poll_new_device running, and
       resets previous_port_serial_number so that poll treats the port as new
       and reopens it if the operator comes back to the type.
       
       Vail Adapter counts as MoMIDI and not serial, so it releases the serial
       side and keeps the MIDI one; a type of None releases both.  The MIDI
       path goes through the existing close_port, keeping the rtmidi guard
       from b8f2319 for a device unplugged while it was selected.
   
    Round the KZD gap toward the ideal, not to nearest
       The adjustment was rounded to the nearest millisecond, so six tenths of
       one moved the value a whole millisecond away from the ideal.  It is
       truncated toward the ideal now -- the ceiling below it, the floor above
       it -- which is math.trunc on the signed adjustment, and the value sent
       is never further from the ideal than rounding put it.
       
       Nothing is lost by not moving: the fraction stays in the carry from
       2a0f491 and is spent once it has grown to a whole millisecond.  Ten
       +0.4 ms nudges still add up to the +4 ms owed, paid where a whole
       millisecond is due rather than dithering by one on every element.
       
       ideal_ms stays a fixed round() of the interval.  Letting the ideal's own
       fraction feed the carry as well diverges: just below a non-integer ideal
       -- 54.545 ms at 22 wpm -- the ceiling lands back above it, and the carry
       grows without settling.
   
    Version 2.0-rc5

    Put the element grid on the heard timeline
       element_timestamp is the heard start of the element now, and the grid
       of element starts hanging off it runs on that timeline.  It cannot
       simply be assigned the heard time, since it is also the base the edges
       are scheduled from -- that would schedule each element a delay late and
       then have the device add another, walking the tone further behind on
       every element.  The edges go in at element_timestamp minus
       sidetone_delay instead, so the device's own delay lands the tone
       exactly on the grid.  The delay is read once per element, so an element
       is consistent with itself.
       
       At 30 wpm with an 18 ms delay, heard element starts come out 80 ms
       apart to within a rounding error, and the first is heard 18 ms after
       keying rather than 36.
       
       Two things follow, both of them the same double count avoided
       elsewhere.  end_sidetone_timestamp loses the sidetone_delay 31df1ee
       added: element_timestamp already carries it, so the heard end is just
       the element's length on top -- it equals end_element_timestamp now, and
       on this timeline those are the same instant.  The mute keeps using the
       keyed start, since it covers the K4's own key-down delay and has
       nothing to do with when the sidetone reaches the operator.
       
       The inter-element sleep now targets a grid sitting sidetone_delay ahead
       of real time, so the keyer waits that much longer after a burst's first
       element.  The offset is constant thereafter and the rate is unchanged.
   
    Version 2.0-rc4

    Time end_sidetone_timestamp from when the sidetone is heard
       It was time.perf_counter() taken after the element's sleep and the KZU
       send -- when the keyer finished, not when the tone ended.  The up edge
       was scheduled for the end of the element and sounds sidetone_delay()
       after that: the displacement measured on the last up edge, which covers
       a clamped edge as well as a lagged one, falling back to the lag before
       any edge has been rendered and to zero with no audio at all.
       
       It stays a perf_counter time.  Its one reader is the auto character and
       word space check, which subtracts it from time.perf_counter(), so both
       sides have to be in the same frame -- the element's start plus a
       duration keeps it there.  Mixing those two frames is what broke the KZD
       gap in 2da5455.
       
       Auto char/word space now measures from when the operator heard the last
       element end rather than from when the keyer finished it, so its
       thresholds trigger later by the sidetone delay.  That is the reference
       the operator is actually judging by, but it is not a no-op.
   
    Hold the KZD gap within a millisecond, spreading the rest
       The adjustment that makes the sent gap follow what the operator hears
       was unbounded, so a lag that moved several milliseconds moved the air
       signal with it.  It is now held to a millisecond either side of the
       ideal, and whatever is left over is carried into the elements that
       follow, a millisecond apiece, rather than dropped: the gaps still add
       up to what the audio rendered, but no single one of them strays far
       enough for a correspondent to hear.
       
       At 30 wpm, a one-off 8 ms stretch comes out as eight gaps of 41 ms
       against an ideal of 40, then back to 40 -- summing to the same +8 ms.
       It works the same downwards, and a debt larger than the burst simply
       keeps paying down.
       
       The carry is kept in seconds, so sub-millisecond adjustments accumulate
       instead of rounding away to nothing.  It resets at a burst's first
       element: what was still owed belongs to gaps already sent, and a new
       burst should not open distorted by them.
   
    Fix the KZD gap measurement: compare like with like
       2da5455 compared this element's down edge as scheduled against the
       previous element's up edge as placed.  next_key_edge places an edge at
       event_time + sidetone_lag, so the difference came out as the gap minus
       the lag: at 30 wpm, a 40 ms inter-element gap was sent as 22 ms with an
       18 ms lag, and any value that went non-positive fell back silently.
       
       The quantity actually wanted is far smaller.  Both edges are placed at
       the time they were scheduled for plus the lag in force, so a constant
       lag shifts a whole burst and leaves every gap inside it exactly as
       intended.  Only a lag that moved between two edges stretches the gap
       between them, and only by however much it moved.
       
       next_key_edge records the up edge's displacement from where it was
       asked for -- which covers a clamped edge as well as a lagged one -- and
       rendered_gap_adjustment answers that against the lag standing now, the
       best estimate of the shift the down edge will get, since it has not
       been rendered when KZD must go out.  A steady lag now adjusts by
       nothing, and the gap can only come out longer than intended, never
       shorter.
       
       Less plumbing than before, not more: schedule_sidetone no longer
       returns anything and placed_up_time is gone.  The shift is cleared in
       reset_sidetone, being meaningless against a stream that has closed.
   
    Measure the KZD inter-element gap on the audio clock
       The gap sent to the K4 was the intended one, inter_element_seconds,
       while the sidetone the operator monitors is whatever the output
       callback rendered.  The two diverge once sidetone_lag is capped and an
       edge has to be taken at the start of a block, so what went on the air
       was not what was heard.
       
       next_key_edge records where it actually placed each up edge, and
       schedule_sidetone answers the stream time it scheduled the down edge
       for; send_element sends the difference between them.  It falls back to
       the intended gap before any up edge has been rendered -- a burst's
       first element -- or if the measurement comes out non-positive or past
       the same 5000 ms bound the first-element path already used.
       
       This reverses the reasoning in 789953a, which kept KZD on perf_counter
       so that the air signal could not follow the audio device; its comment
       saying so is corrected.  KZU's element length is still the intended
       one: it cannot be measured until after the element has been rendered.
       
       On a clean stream nothing changes -- intended and rendered gaps agree
       and the up edge moves not at all -- so the two only part company when
       an edge is genuinely displaced.
   
    Schedule an element's sidetone edges ahead of the blocks carrying them
       An edge that reaches the output callback before the block carrying it
       has been filled is placed on its exact frame.  One reported as it
       happens has already missed that block, and sidetone_lag exists to drag
       it forward -- which is where the monitoring delay comes from, and where
       the quantizing of elements to the blocksize comes from once that lag is
       capped.  A keyer knows both of an element's edges before it sends it,
       so only a burst's first down edge is genuinely unpredictable.
       
       send_element hands both edges to Audio.schedule_sidetone at the top of
       the element, before sleeping for it: element_timestamp already holds
       this element's intended start, so the audio side gets the whole
       inter-element gap of notice, and the element itself for the up edge.
       The two clocks are read together, once per element, so drift between
       them cannot accumulate.  KZD/KZU stay on perf_counter -- what goes on
       the air must not follow the audio device.  start_sidetone/stop_sidetone
       are untouched for the straight key, which can predict nothing.
       
       Verified at unit level: both edges queued in order, element length
       exact across the clock conversion, nothing queued and nothing raised
       with no stream or the sidetone disabled, straight key unchanged.  A
       clean stream behaves exactly as before, same lag and no rhythm error.
       
       Not verified under slips, which is the case it is meant to help: the
       simulation measures intended times on the wall clock and heard times on
       the block index, and a slipping DAC timeline separates the two, so its
       numbers there cannot be told apart from that artifact.
   
    Version 2.0-rc3

    Derive the sidetone lag cap from the device
       The cap is what is left as monitoring delay once a slip has pushed the
       lag up, and 50ms was a guess rather than anything about the device.
       The lag only ever has to cover the gap between an edge being keyed and
       the first block that can still carry it: the stream's own output
       latency, plus a block for where between two callbacks the edge landed.
       
       Over 60 seconds of dits on a 10ms device with 8ms blocks, the residual
       goes from a flat 50ms to 26ms.  A clean stream settles at 18ms either
       way -- latency plus a block, the floor below which the delay is the
       device's, not ours -- so nothing changes where this was already fine.
       
       Floored at a block, since a host API reporting no latency at all would
       otherwise cap below what the device needs and land every edge on a
       block boundary.
   
    Version 2.0-rc2

    Wait for the monitor thread before dropping the process
       exited is the last thing monitor_loop does, so run() has not returned
       when that queued signal reaches the Qt thread.  on_exited then pops the
       process from the table, which drops the last reference to it --
       writer_loop's bound method held the other one until _close_pipe()
       released it two lines earlier -- and destroying a QThread that is still
       running is a Qt fatal.  stop() already waits on the parent-initiated
       path; the path where the child exits on its own did not.
       
       Hardening, not a confirmed fix: the race would not reproduce here even
       with the window widened deliberately, so whether it is what aborts on
       Windows is unproven.  The wait returns immediately in the normal case,
       the thread being already on its way out.
   
    Stop losing Qt's diagnostics, fatals included
       _qt_message_handler wrote to sys.stderr unguarded, and a windowed build
       has no stderr at all: sys.stderr is None under pythonw and in a
       PyInstaller windowed bundle, so it raised on every Qt message.  PyQt
       cannot propagate that back into C++, so the exception is swallowed and
       the message goes with it.
       
       Qt's own fatals abort the process, and this handler is the only place
       their reason is ever written -- so the app died with nothing printed,
       which is exactly how the panadapter-at-startup crash presents.  Checked
       against real PyQt6: a QThread destroyed while running aborts either
       way, but only a working handler says so.
       
       It writes through the module's own stderr now, for the reason that
       exists (it survives fd 2 being closed), inside a try that cannot let
       anything escape.  The QThreadStorage suppression is unchanged.
   
    Schedule the unmute on output frames, not a per-element thread
       send_element built a threading.Timer for every element, which is a
       whole OS thread each time: 96us of thread creation on the keyer thread
       at the median here, 268us for the first one in the process, and 1.3ms
       at worst.  It ran immediately before start_sidetone, and thread
       creation contends for the GIL the output callback needs to meet its
       deadline -- first-sidetone weighted, because the first thread of the
       process is the dear one, which is why warming the render path only
       reduced the underruns rather than ending them.
       
       mute_k4 takes the delay itself now and records the frame to lift the
       mute at; the output callback lifts it on the way past.  Creating 25
       elements went from 26 threads to 1.
       
       unmute_k4 clears a pending deadline, so a new mute replaces it the way
       timer.cancel() did, and reset_sidetone clears it too -- a frame
       belonging to a stream that has been replaced never arrives, and the
       mute would stick.
       
       The unmute is quantized to the output block now rather than millisecond
       wall clock.  It lands within one block and never early, and the six
       chunks k4_mute_count drops afterwards are far coarser than that.
   
    Warm the sidetone render path before opening the stream
       mix_sidetone does not run until something is keyed, so the first key
       down is the first time the output callback executes any of it:
       sidetone_block_ramp allocates its array there and every numpy call
       below it runs cold.  On a fresh process that block measures around
       three times a settled one -- 130us against 45us -- which is enough to
       miss a deadline on a device with little margin, and is why the underrun
       only ever turned up on the first sidetone.
       
       warm_sidetone renders one throwaway block through the real code rather
       than an imitation that could drift away from it.  It runs before the
       stream is opened, so no callback is competing for the state it touches,
       and the reset_sidetone() already after the open clears what it leaves.
       
       Warmed, the first keyed block costs about what any block carrying a key
       edge costs, and comes out sample for sample identical.
   
    Raise output buffer time to .008

    Reduce muting to 4 packets beyond Key Down Initial Delay

    Cap sidetone_lag so it cannot ratchet up while keying
       sidetone_lag only ever grows: the one += is in next_key_edge, and the
       only thing that lowers it is the reset in mix_sidetone, which wants
       half a second of quiet.  Continuous keying never gives it that.
       Each output underrun slips the DAC timeline ahead of the clock the
       keyer thread reads, the next edge then computes a negative offset, and
       another block goes on the lag.  Windows underruns where Linux and macOS
       mostly do not, so the lag there was very noticeable.
       
       Past max_sidetone_lag the lag is pinned and the edge is taken at the
       start of the current block rather than carried forward.  It is now
       off by at most one block and never accumulates.
   
    Do inter-element sleep at the beginning of elements

    Drop K4 audio chunks while muted, not only after
       The drop was guarded by "not self.k4_muted", so a chunk pulled while
       muted was played rather than dropped.
       
       Muting now silences on the block it is asked for, and the count still
       drops its chunks afterwards.
   
    Remove the K4 audio fade shaping
       A block the queue came up short on is plain silence now rather than the
       last sample that went out decaying away, and sidetone_shape is back to
       the five fields the sidetone itself needs.
   
    Survive a midi device removed out from under us
       Unplugging the device can leave the Windows MM port in a state where
       closing it fails.
       
       Route every close through MidiInput.close_port(), which swallows
       rtmidi.RtMidiError: the base of SystemError and the rest, so a
       differently worded WinMM failure is covered too.
   
    Version 2.0-rc1

    Correctly match similar midi input/output names

    Version 1.6.17.20

