    Version 1.6.17.16

    Send numpy arrays to portaudio instead of bytes
       RawOutputStream/RawInputStream become OutputStream/InputStream, so the
       callback's outdata is a (frames, channels) float32 array and read()
       returns one.  The output queues and the sidetone packets carry flat
       interleaved float32 arrays now rather than bytes, which drops the
       tobytes()/frombuffer() round trips between them.  The opus calls still
       take and return bytes -- that is the codec's boundary, not portaudio's.
       
       sidetone_full_cycle_packet had used bytes *= full_cycles to repeat its
       buffer, which on an array would scale the samples instead; numpy.tile()
       gives byte-identical output.
       
       write_outdata() replaces the duplicated ljust() padding, reshaping the
       flat samples into portaudio's block and zero-filling what a queue
       underrun came up short by.
       
       previous_outdata held the whole outdata buffer and was read as [-8:] on
       a later callback.  It is previous_output_frame now, and holds a copy:
       outdata is a frombuffer() view of portaudio's own memory and is only
       valid for the duration of the callback it arrives in.  It is also
       initialized, which it never was -- a start_sidetone() before the first
       output block raised AttributeError inside the realtime callback, and
       now fades from silence.
   
