    Version 1.6.17.11

    Explicitly wrap keyed elements with TX/RX calls

    Send larger sidetone audio packets

    Key the K4 before send sidetone audio

    Suppress stderr on sounddevice open

    Widen comboboxes on MacOS

    Stop clipping nested group box titles
       qt_group_box() pairs qt_tight_groupbox_stylesheet() with
       qt_size_tight_groupbox_title(), because under the CSS box model that
       stylesheet switches to, a QGroupBox's own width no longer covers its
       title.  Stylesheets cascade, so the boxes nested inside "Keyer Settings"
       -- built as bare QGroupBox(...) rather than through qt_group_box() --
       pick up the stylesheet from their ancestor but not the sizing that goes
       with it.  Where their content is narrower than their title, the last
       character is cut off: "Dit Paddle Note", "Dah Paddle Note", "Dit Paddle
       Signal", "Dah Paddle Signal", "Dit Mem %", "Dah Mem %".
       
       Size them the same way, and again at the setTitle() calls that swap in
       the wider "Straight Key Note"/"Straight Key Signal".
   
    Keep the most recent K4 audio packets
       Discard the oldest packets when necessary.
   
    Version 1.6.17.10

    Unmute after sending sidetone on K4->RX
       After sending a sidetone, we don't unmute until the K4 tells us that
       it's in receive mode.
   
    Drain output queues on unmute

    Drain audio output queue as well

    Remove Received Audio Latency setting
       The K4's Remote Audio Latency setting is all we need.
   
    No longer split up the K4's received audio packets

    Drain leftover audio bytes as well as output queue

    Hold keyer device warnings until CW mode is entered
       A missing or unopenable paddle/key device is only actionable while the
       operator is actually keying, so route MidiInput's, VailAdapter's and
       SerialInput's warnings through KeyerWarning: shown at once when the K4
       is in CW or CW-R, otherwise queued and shown on entering it.
       
       These polls start about a second after launch, so the usual case is
       that the K4 hasn't been connected to yet.  Subscribing to the mode
       responses covers both ways CW is arrived at afterwards -- the operator
       switching into it, and the mode simply showing up in the initial
       parameter dump because the K4 was already there.
       
       For that second case tx_mode() has to answer before the dump completes,
       where the mode parameter may still be missing; return '' rather than
       raising on int(None).  '' is already the tuple's own "unknown mode"
       value and both callers handle it.
       
       Clear the queue where the fault clears (the device turned up, or the
       port opened), so a problem that has since gone away isn't announced on
       entering CW as though it were still there.
   
    Rename Devices tab to Audio

    Move Keyer Settings to a new Keying tab in the CW window
       Add a "Keying" tab between the CW and Configure tabs, and move the
       "Keyer Settings" group there from the Devices tab.
       
       The keyer methods move from AppSettings_widget to CW, which builds the
       group in __init__ rather than in add_keying_tab(): constructing those
       widgets is also what configures the keyer hardware, and add_keying_tab()
       does not run until the window is first opened.
       
       AppSettings_widget's private _show()/_hide() go with them -- only the
       keyer code used them, and CW inherits identical ones from TextButtons.
       
       Give the cw item selectbg: pink, as appsettings had, so the "Generate
       Sidetone?" dropdown keeps its popup highlight color.
   
