    Panadapter: raise an already-open window when Pan is clicked again
       open_window() returned silently when the window was already open, so
       clicking Pan while it sat buried behind something else appeared to do
       nothing. The window belongs to the subprocess, so the parent cannot
       raise it directly and instead asks it to raise itself, via a new
       no-payload message type. That goes through send()'s blocking path
       rather than the drop-when-full one PAN/MINI-PAN frames use, so a click
       cannot be lost to a queue momentarily full of spectrum data.
       
       The child restores the window first if it is minimized, so it comes
       back rather than being restacked while still minimized, then raises and
       activates it. Whether the activate actually takes focus is up to the
       window manager -- some refuse it from a process that owns no focused
       window, which is this case exactly, since the click landed on the main
       window in the parent process.
       
