       Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
   
    Panadapter: fix stretched frequency scale -- sample_rate is kHz, not Hz/bin
       The PAN sub-header's sample_rate field was read as the frequency
       resolution (Hz per bin). It is really the complex sample rate in kHz,
       with the frame's full bin set forming one FFT across exactly that rate,
       so the resolution is sample_rate * 1000 / full_bin_count. For the one
       set of values confirmed against real hardware (1024 bins, sample_rate
       192) that is a standard 192 kHz rate over a 1024-point FFT: 187.5 Hz
       per bin spanning 192 kHz, not 192 Hz per bin spanning ~197 kHz.
       
       Reading it as Hz/bin scaled every offset from the center by
       1024/1000 = 2.4%, so the trace was correct at center_hz and
       progressively wrong toward both edges: about 2.3 kHz off at the edges
       of a 200 kHz span, 1.2 kHz at 100 kHz, and a signal truly at 740 kHz
       drawn at 740.96 kHz.
       
       Both push_frame and push_minipan now derive hz_per_bin from each frame
       and use it for the crop, the span and the bin positions, so this stays
       correct if the radio sends a different rate or bin count. The mini-pan
       was wrong by the same factor all along, but its span is narrow enough
       (10 kHz, or 2 kHz in CW) that it only amounted to ~120 Hz at the edges,
       which is why the error showed up on the trace first.
       
       Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
   
    Panadapter: fix per-bin frequencies, VFO-step tuning, mini-pan smoothing
       - Fix the bin-to-frequency mapping used by both the trace and the
         mini-pan. numpy.linspace(left, right, bin_count) divides the span
         into bin_count-1 intervals for bin_count points, so each point landed
         span/(bin_count-1) apart rather than the true sample_rate (Hz per
         bin) apart, and the two outermost bins were forced onto the nominal
         edges instead of sitting half a bin inside them. Bin i's frequency is
         now computed directly as left_edge + (i + 0.5) * sample_rate. The
         view range, labels and waterfall image rect still span the full
         nominal edges, so only the data points moved.
       
       - Round a trace click's frequency to the receiver's current VFO tuning
         step before sending it, so click-to-tune lands on the same
         frequencies the tuning knob would. VT;/VT$; are now tracked by the
         parent (which is the only side that sends the command), decoded the
         same way VFO.on_step_received already does.
       
       - Apply the trace's 3-frame rolling per-bin average to the mini-pan as
         well, factored out of _trace_average into a shared _rolling_average,
         and scale the mini-pan's range to 1.5x rather than 2x the low-to-peak
         height so peaks read 2/3 of the way up instead of halfway.
       
