       Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
   
    Remove green background from the "Test Off" button

    Halve bargraph height, keep width unchanged
       Adds a thicknesspercent option to Bargraph that scales the bar's
       thickness (height for horizontal, width for vertical) relative to
       Qt's natural style-driven size, the same way the existing
       lengthpercent scales its length relative to the bottomtext width.
       Set to 50 on all six bargraphs (both S-meters, RF Out, ALC, CMP, SWR).
       
       A previous attempt at "half size" halved lengthpercent instead, which
       shrank width instead of height and left height untouched -- reverted
       that and used thicknesspercent instead.
   
    Sync placeholder config_data with example_config_files/k4companion.yaml
       Picks up the app-version label going single-line, matching the real
       config.
   
    Fix QGroupBox titles clipping on the right when wider than their content
       qt_tight_groupbox_stylesheet() switches a QGroupBox to the CSS box
       model to tighten its title area, but once that happens the box's own
       sizeHint() no longer widens to fit the title the way native title
       rendering did. Any box whose content is narrower than its title --
       "QSK" wrapping a small toggle, "K4-Companion Version" as a label:
       option, and others -- then clips the title text against its right
       edge.
       
       qt_size_tight_groupbox_title() forces a minimum width covering the
       title plus the stylesheet's own title offset/padding/border, applied
       everywhere the tight stylesheet is: qt_group_box() (used throughout
       the app) and label_and_leading_frame() (the generic label: option).
       Multi-line titles are unaffected -- they already bypass the tight
       stylesheet entirely via the existing '\n' in title check.
   
    Make all labels only contain a single line

    Move Tx:/Sub Rx: label left padding out of the widget's own padding
       The previous padleft implementation added padding-left on the QLabel's
       own stylesheet, which extends the label's colored background into that
       space -- not a true gap. Move it to the outer frame's contentsMargins
       (left side only) instead, so it's real blank space before the widget
       rather than padding within it.
   
    Add left padding to the Tx: and Sub Rx: antenna labels
       Adds a padleft option to Text, applied via padding-left on the
       QLabel's own stylesheet rather than the existing (symmetric) padx
       option, so it doesn't also push the label's own adjacent widget to
       the right. Set to 10px on the "Tx:" and "Sub Rx:" labels to visually
       separate them from the antenna dropdown before each one.
   
    Fix erratic mouse wheel behavior during rapid rotation
       mouse_wheel_mult() computed direction as `1 if delta > 0 else -1`, so
       any wheel event with angleDelta().y() == 0 fell into the else branch
       and was treated as a step backwards. That's a documented Qt corner
       case -- e.g. the tail end of a kinetic scroll, or a leftover sub-notch
       remainder from a high-resolution wheel spun quickly -- and exactly the
       kind of event more likely to show up under rapid rotation, which is
       what made fast scrolling look like it was randomly reversing.
       
       All three callers (VFO/RIT/Slider on_mouse_wheel) already no-op safely
       on a zero step, so no other changes were needed.
   
    Size the Main Rx/Tx/Sub Rx antenna labels to match their combobox's height
       Text widgets placed a QLabel with Qt.AlignmentFlag.AlignTop at both the
       outer-frame and inner-label layers, which shrink-wraps to the label's
       own sizeHint() and anchors it to the top of its grid cell -- even when
       the row is already as tall as a taller sibling widget (the antenna
       comboboxes) sharing that row.
       
       Extend the existing sticky: option (already supported by Group for
       'center') with a new 'fill' value that passes no alignment flags,
       letting the widget stretch to fill its cell instead. Text now accepts
       sticky: too, using it for both the outer_frame placement and its own
       label placement.
       
       The actual layout config lives in example_config_files/k4companion.yaml
       (the config_data string embedded in k4companion itself is just a
       placeholder/fallback), so that's where sticky: fill is set on the
       three antenna label definitions.
   
    Let the main window come to the front of the CW and Macros windows
       Both windows are created as QDialog(Page.top), and QDialog's default
       window type (Dialog) is treated by most window managers as
       transient-for-parent, forcing it to always stay above Page.top.
       Passing Qt.WindowType.Window overrides that so they behave as
       independent top-level windows instead.
   
    Use the platform's default monospace font instead of hardcoding DejaVu Sans Mono
       DejaVu Sans Mono is commonly bundled on Linux but isn't guaranteed to
       be installed on Windows or macOS. QFontDatabase.systemFont(FixedFont)
       resolves to whatever monospace font the OS actually provides
       (Consolas, Menlo/SF Mono, etc.), keeping the fixed-pitch alignment
       this font is used for working on every platform.
   
    Size Menu QListWidgets to their widest item, not the layout's full width
       Both the K4 menu-entry list (Menu.listbox) and a menu entry's own
       value-picker list (Menu_definition.listbox) left width unconstrained,
       so QListWidget stretched to fill whatever space the layout offered.
       Height was already fixed to fit the content; do the same for width by
       measuring the widest item with QFontMetrics, mirroring the tkinter
       version's listbox.configure(width=...) sized to the longest
       description.
   
    Move app version to column 1 of the server tab

    Remove remaining tkinter vestiges
       The PyQt6 port is complete: every item type in the widget dispatch
       table (Container.show_widgets) maps to a real Qt class, and the else
       branch raises on unknown types rather than falling back to a
       placeholder. So the unused Placeholder class, the commented-out
       tkinter imports, and the stale NOTE describing the now-finished
       transition can all go.
       
       Along the way, fix a real bug that shared that comment block: `import
       traceback` was commented out too, even though traceback.print_exc()
       is called from two exception handlers elsewhere in the file -- both
       would have raised NameError if they'd ever actually triggered.
   
    Change default connection port from 9205 to 9204
       Now that TLS/PSK on 9204 works correctly, prefer it as the default so
       new connections are encrypted unless a different port is explicitly
       configured.
   
    Fix TLS/PSK connection on port 9204
       The PSK was hardcoded to a placeholder ('1234567890') instead of the
       K4's actual password, and no PSK identity was sent -- both would
       cause the K4 to reject the handshake outright (observed as
       [SSL: UNEXPECTED_EOF_WHILE_READING]). Also pin the cipher list to the
       specific PSK suites the K4-Remote protocol draft specifies (in its
       priority order) instead of the generic, much broader 'PSK' cipher
       string, and set minimum_version alongside the existing maximum_version
       so the negotiated range is TLS 1.2 exactly, not open-ended below it.
       
       Modeled on the k4-panadapter project's from-scratch K4-Remote
       implementation, which derives the PSK from the same password used for
       port 9205's plaintext auth.
       
       Also fixes a second bug this would have hit next: after a successful
       TLS handshake, the code went on to also send a SHA-384 password
       digest -- port 9205's plaintext auth mechanism. For port 9204 the
       TLS-PSK handshake itself is the authentication, so that digest-send
       is now skipped when connecting via TLS.
   
    Version 1.6.16.3

    Fix Main/Sub Rx antenna showing "None" at startup
       Dropdown_Widget.config() rebuilds the combobox's item list via clear()
       + addItems(), which makes Qt auto-select item 0, and only restored the
       previously-displayed text when it happened to match one of the new
       items. Antenna's main/sub dropdowns can legitimately display text
       that's never an item (the "=TX ANT" substitution in on_k4_select_rx),
       so that guard always failed for them.
       
       At startup, Antenna subscribes to AR;/AR$; (setting the correct
       display) and then immediately subscribes to ACM;/ACS; right after --
       both already cached by the time the initial parameter dump completes,
       so ACM/ACS fires synchronously too and calls set_values_rx() again,
       wiping the just-set antenna back to the repopulated list's first item,
       "None".
       
       ttk.Combobox's displayed text was never tied to its 'values' option in
       the tkinter version, so restore current text unconditionally (skipping
       only the very first, empty-text population) rather than gating on
       list membership.
   
    Version v1.6.16.2

    Fix Main/Sub Rx antenna selectors not updating from radio-side changes
       Antenna's on_k4_select_rx substitutes a display string (tx_short_names)
       that isn't necessarily one of the rx dropdown's own items whenever the
       selected rx antenna matches the tx antenna (e.g. "=TX ANT"). The
       tkinter version tolerated this via ttk.Combobox.set(), which displays
       arbitrary text regardless of the configured values. QComboBox's
       setCurrentText() has no such tolerance: on a readonly combobox it's a
       silent no-op unless the text matches an existing item, so those
       updates were dropped.
       
       Adding the mismatched text as a real combobox item (an earlier attempt
       at this fix) isn't right either -- it becomes a second, bogus
       selectable entry, and picking it crashed on_select's
       rx_short_names.index(value) lookup since the synthetic text was never
       a real name.
       
       Dropdown_Widget now takes a display_only flag, used only by Antenna's
       main/sub dropdowns, that makes the combobox editable with a read-only
       line edit standing in for it. This decouples the displayed text from
       the selectable items entirely -- setCurrentText() can show anything
       without adding a pickable option -- while forwarding clicks to
       showPopup() keeps the click-anywhere-opens-the-list behavior intact.
   
    Fix VFO/RIT digit field: clicking a digit didn't move the text cursor
       mousePressEvent intercepted left-clicks that matched a tuning-step
       column to fire the step-selection callback, but returned before
       letting QLineEdit place the cursor there. In the tkinter version the
       tag-click handler for step selection ran alongside the Text widget's
       own click handling rather than instead of it, so clicking a digit both
       selected its step and positioned the cursor for backspace/insert
       editing. Falling through to super() restores that.
   
    Tighten UI padding/sizing throughout, fix VFO width and antenna-label color bugs
       - Add a global CompactStyle (QProxyStyle) tightening the default layout
         margins/spacing every QGridLayout/QVBoxLayout/QHBoxLayout falls back
         to when not set explicitly.
       - DigitEntry (VFO/RIT readout) never overrode sizeHint(), so it used
         Qt's generic ~17-character default -- at the configured 31pt font
         that produced a box far wider than its actual digit_width content.
         Added a sizeHint() override sized to what's actually displayed.
       - label_and_leading_frame() and qt_group_box() (factored into a shared
         qt_tight_groupbox_stylesheet() helper): reduced inner content margins
         to 0, and tightened QGroupBox's native title-to-content gap via a
         stylesheet (margin-top sized to the label's actual font-metric
         height) instead of Qt's much more generous built-in reservation.
         Multi-line labels are excluded, since they measure and clip
         unpredictably under the CSS box model.
       - Gave SliderWidget/Equalizers/Menu_definition sliders explicit
         pageStep(1) so a groove click moves one resolution unit instead of
         jumping a default page of 10.
       - DropdownWidget (Band/Mode/BW/etc.): reduced the minimum-width buffer
         and tightened QComboBox's native padding/drop-down arrow width.
       - Reduced entries_displayed for the Menu tab's listbox from 24 to 21.
       - Fixed a real color bug: Page.top_bg was snapshotted from Default.bg
         in main() *before* read_config() applied the YAML's real bg override,
         so 'bg: None' widgets (the Main/Tx/Sub Rx antenna labels) resolved
         against the pristine class default 'maroon1' -- a Tk-only color name
         Qt's stylesheet parser can't resolve, silently rendering black.
         Removed the stale snapshot; 'bg: None' now resolves directly against
         the live Default.bg.
       
       Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
   
    Fix descending-range sliders, groove-click page step, and Macros startup visibility race
       - SliderWidget: setMinimum/setMaximum were fed raw from_/to values
         directly, so descending configs (e.g. AF Limiter's from:30/to:1) set
         minimum > maximum, an invalid QSlider range that Qt silently
         collapses, leaving the slider stuck. Compute numeric lo/hi instead
         and use setInvertedAppearance/setInvertedControls only where needed
         to preserve tk.Scale's from_-at-top/left convention.
       - Give SliderWidget, Equalizers, and Menu_definition sliders an
         explicit pageStep of 1 -- QSlider's default of 10 made a groove
         click (vs. dragging the handle) jump 10 resolution units instead of
         tk.Scale's one-unit trough click.
       - Macros.open() no longer unconditionally shows the window. It raced
         against the show_or_hide() call made right after it in
         on_received_initial_parameters, which could leave a still-forming,
         empty window visible at startup when Macros was disabled. open() now
         only builds content; show()/hide() are the sole callers of
         top_level.show()/hide().
       
       Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
   
    Add numeric value labels to sliders; fix vertical slider inversion and
       
       Adds a value readout to SliderWidget (honoring the showvalue config
       option, defaulting to shown as tk.Scale did), Menu_definition's
       slider, and each Equalizers band fader -- matching tk.Scale's built-in
       showvalue display that had been dropped during the port.
       
       Fixes found via real K4 hardware testing:
       - Equalizers' and Menu_definition's vertical sliders were rendering
         upside down. Both had setInvertedAppearance(True)/
         setInvertedControls(True), based on a wrong assumption that Qt's
         default vertical QSlider needed inversion to match tk.Scale's
         from_=high(top)/to=low(bottom) fader convention. Verified
         empirically that a default (uninverted) vertical QSlider already
         puts its maximum at the top, same as tk.Scale -- the inversion was
         flipping it backwards. Removed from both.
       - The AF Gain sliders (main/sub) use evallength to size themselves to
         another widget's current rendered width. That gets evaluated in
         on_received_initial_parameters(), which fires synchronously while
         connecting to the K4 -- and start() (which connects) runs entirely
         before top.show() in main(). tkinter's winfo_width() forces an
         immediate layout pass on demand even pre-mainloop, so this worked
         originally; Qt's width() just returns a cached value, 0 for
         anything never shown or laid out, collapsing the sliders to
         effectively nothing. Deferred the width-dependent resize with
         QTimer.singleShot(0, ...) so it runs after the event loop has
         actually processed the window's show/layout events.
       
       Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
   
    Port GUI from tkinter to PyQt6
       Replaces the entire tkinter-based UI (~24 dynamic widget classes driven
       by the YAML config framework) with PyQt6 equivalents, including a
       from-scratch digit-editor widget for VFO/RIT (no direct Qt equivalent
       exists) and a QDialog-based CW window with an application-wide event
       filter reproducing "type anywhere to key" behavior. Poll is
       reimplemented on QTimer; the existing queue-based thread/GUI handoff
       pattern carries over unchanged and remains safe.
       
       Fixes found via real K4 hardware testing during the port:
       - Window could get permanently stuck too small: setFixedSize() locked
         geometry before async content (Menu's list, AppSettings' dynamic
         panels) populated; replaced with a continuously auto-fitting layout
         constraint.
       - Server.receive_non_audio_packets()/dequeue_k4_cat() only rescheduled
         themselves after successfully finishing, so any exception while
         processing a response permanently killed all further inbound
         processing (while outbound sending, on a separate path, kept
         working) -- now wrapped so a bad response is logged and skipped
         instead of stopping the receive loop.
       - SliderWidget.on_response_received() called setMinimum()/setMaximum()
         before blocking signals; clamping the slider's current position
         fired valueChanged and echoed a just-received value back to the K4
         as a command, which could trigger a request/response loop.
       - Menu_definition slider construction crashed for menu type 'REF',
         which intentionally keeps low/high/current as raw strings so the
         value can be echoed back to the K4 unmodified; the slider widget
         itself needs numeric bounds, fixed by converting only at the
         Qt-facing call sites.
       - CW's application-wide key event filter crashed on non-QWidget
         objects (e.g. QWindow) since QWidget.isAncestorOf() only accepts a
         QWidget.
       - Widget.add_tab() registered a tab's name after calling addTab(), but
         Qt fires currentChanged synchronously for the first tab added --
         current_tabname was silently left unset, so typed characters in CW's
         immediate-tx field were dropped with no error.
       - CW.tx_poll_interval started as None until the K4's keyer speed
         response arrived; typing or sending a macro before then crashed
         Poll.call() with a None interval.
       
       Also fixes several bugs pre-existing in the tkinter version, unrelated
       to the toolkit but found while porting the surrounding code: a
       self.blink attribute/method name collision in Radiobutton, a
       `sef.font()` typo in Getvalue, self.cat_only referencing a nonexistent
       attribute in AppSettings_widget, and manually typing a RIT offset
       always silently reverting due to an undefined variable inside a bare
       except clause.
       
       Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
   
